window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("1john", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"1 John","chapters":"5","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Light, Love, and the Assurance of Eternal Life","paragraphs":["Picture a church in turmoil. A group of its own members — some of them gifted teachers — have walked out. Before they left, they taught that they had a higher knowledge of God; that the eternal Christ could never truly have come in a body of flesh; that what you do with your body does not matter to your spirit; that they had moved beyond sin. Those who stayed are shaken. Were the leavers right? Do we really know God? Are we really saved?","Into that confusion an old man writes a letter. He does not open with his name. He opens with what he has seen and touched: “That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life” (1 John 1:1). He is one of the original eyewitnesses, and he writes to give his ‘little children’ certainty: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (1 John 5:13).","The letter circles around three tests, again and again, like a spiral. The test of truth: do you confess that Jesus is the Christ who came in the flesh? The test of obedience: do you keep His commandments and walk as He walked? And the test of love: do you love your brothers and sisters? Those who claim to know God but fail these tests are deceived; those who pass them can be sure. Over everything stand two great statements about God: “God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (1 John 1:5), and “God is love” (1 John 4:8).","The letter names no author, but from the earliest times the church has received it as the work of John the apostle, the son of Zebedee, the disciple whom Jesus loved. Its language and themes — light and darkness, truth and lies, life, love, remaining, being born of God — are unmistakably those of the Gospel of John. Tradition places John in Ephesus at the end of his long life, an old man among the churches of Asia, still repeating one message: love one another. The Son of Thunder (Mark 3:17) had become the apostle of love."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"2 Peter","intro":"2 Peter ended by pointing ahead to 1 John and 2 John. Peter's last letter warned against false teachers and called believers to grow in the knowledge of Christ; John's first letter shows how to tell true knowledge from false, and true believers from false ones.","points":[{"title":"From Peter the Eyewitness to John the Eyewitness","body":"Peter wrote, “we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (2 Peter 1:16). John begins in the same place, with what was heard, seen and touched (1 John 1:1). The two fishermen who ran together to the empty tomb both anchor the church in the same reality: the Son of God was not an idea or a vision, but a man of flesh and blood whom they knew."},{"title":"From False Teachers to Antichrists","body":"Peter warned, “as false teachers will also be among you” (2 Peter 2:1). John says they have arrived: “Little children, these are the end times, and as you heard that the Antichrist is coming, even now many antichrists have arisen” (1 John 2:18). Peter exposed their greed and immorality; John exposes their theology: they deny that Jesus is the Christ who has come in the flesh. And John gives the church tests to recognise them: “Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God” (1 John 4:1)."},{"title":"From ‘Grow in Knowledge’ to ‘That You May Know’","body":"Peter's last words were, “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18). John's letter is about knowing: the word ‘know’ appears over thirty times. False teachers claimed secret knowledge; John says every believer knows God through Christ, and he gives clear signs by which they can know that they know Him (2:3). The goal is not mystery but assurance."},{"title":"From ‘Beware’ to ‘Remain in Him’","body":"Peter's final warning was, “beware, lest being carried away with the error of the wicked, you fall from your own steadfastness” (2 Peter 3:17). John's answer is one word repeated through the letter: μένω, remain, abide, continue. “Now, little children, remain in him, that when he appears, we may have boldness, and not be ashamed before him at his coming” (1 John 2:28). The defence against being carried away is staying close to Christ."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"God Is Light, God Is Love — So That You May Know","anchorRef":"1 John 1:5; 4:8; 5:13","paragraphs":["John states the nature of God twice in the letter, and both statements shape everything. First: “God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (1 John 1:5). God is pure, true and holy; those who claim fellowship with Him must walk in the light, confessing their sin rather than denying it. Second: “He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love” (1 John 4:8). God's very nature is self-giving love, shown supremely in the cross: “In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins” (1 John 4:10).","Because God is light, the believer's life must be marked by obedience and honesty about sin. Because God is love, the believer's life must be marked by love for others: “Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God; and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God” (1 John 4:7). Light and love are not two separate tests; they are two sides of knowing the same God.","And because God has revealed Himself in His Son, the believer can be sure. John's purpose statement comes near the end: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (1 John 5:13). His Gospel was written so that people might believe (John 20:31); his letter is written so that believers might know. Assurance is not arrogance; it is resting on God's own testimony about His Son (5:11–12).","These themes run through all of John's writings. The Gospel begins, “In the beginning was the Word” (John 1:1) and the letter begins, “That which was from the beginning” (1 John 1:1). Jesus gave the new commandment in the upper room: “A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another” (John 13:34). John, who leaned against Jesus' breast at that supper, spent the rest of his life repeating it."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 5 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Jesus Really Came in the Flesh","body":"The heart of the Christian faith is not an idea but a person who could be seen, heard and touched. “every spirit who confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God” (1 John 4:2). His real body, real blood and real death are the ground of forgiveness: “the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (1 John 1:7). Any teaching that makes Jesus less than fully God or less than fully human is not of God."},{"title":"True Faith Shows in Obedience and Love","body":"John will not allow a faith that only talks. “One who says, ‘I know him,’ and doesn’t keep his commandments, is a liar” (1 John 2:4), and “My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth” (1 John 3:18). Those who have been born of God will show it — imperfectly, but really — by turning from sin and loving others."},{"title":"You Can Know You Have Eternal Life","body":"God does not want His children to live in uncertainty. “He who has the Son has the life. He who doesn’t have God’s Son doesn’t have the life” (1 John 5:12). When you trust in Jesus, confess your sin, keep His word and love His people, you have the evidence of new birth. “We love him, because he first loved us” (1 John 4:19)."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Are You Sure?","body":"Do you know that you have eternal life, or do you hope so, fear not, or simply never think about it? John writes so that you may know. As you read, ask the Holy Spirit to show you the evidence of His life in you — and to expose anything that is only talk."},{"title":"Who Is Hard for You to Love?","body":"John will say that whoever hates his brother is still in the darkness. Name the person in your church or family whom you find hardest to love. Bring that name to God before you begin, and read 1 John with that relationship open before Him."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, You are light, and in You is no darkness at all. You are love, and You showed Your love by sending Your Son as the atoning sacrifice for my sins. As I open the first letter of John, I thank You for the eyewitnesses who heard, saw and touched the Word of life, and for the eternal life that was with You and was revealed to us. Bring me into deeper fellowship with You and with Your Son, Jesus Christ. Where I walk in darkness, bring me into the light. Where I deny my sin, make me honest to confess it, and thank You that You are faithful and righteous to forgive me and cleanse me. Teach me to keep Your commandments and to walk as Jesus walked. Fill me with love for my brothers and sisters, in deed and in truth. Give me discernment to test the spirits and to hold to the truth that Jesus Christ came in the flesh. And let me know, with settled assurance, that I have eternal life in Your Son. In Jesus' name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 John","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"The Word of Life and Walking in the Light","themeTitle":"“God Is Light, and in Him Is No Darkness at All”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life","understanding":"ὃ ἦν ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς, “That which was from the beginning” — ἀρχή, beginning, echoing ‘In the beginning was the Word’ (John 1:1); the word is also in the power family, where it means rule, but here it has its first sense. Then three verbs of the senses, rising in intensity: ἀκούω, “heard”; ὁράω, “seen with our eyes” — from the see family: to see and perceive; θεάομαι, “saw” — also from the see family: to gaze at, to contemplate over time, as an audience watches a play. Finally ψηλαφάω, “our hands touched” — to feel, to handle, the word Jesus used: ‘Touch me and see’ (Luke 24:39). περὶ τοῦ λόγου τῆς ζωῆς, “concerning the Word of life” — λόγος, from the word family: Christ Himself as God's Word; ζωή, from the life family.","words":[{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G2300","hebrew":"θεάομαι","translit":"theáomai","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G5584","hebrew":"ψηλαφάω","translit":"psēlapháō","english":"touched"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":2,"text":"(and the life was revealed, and we have seen, and testify, and declare to you the life, the eternal life, which was with the Father, and was revealed to us);","understanding":"καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἐφανερώθη, “and the life was revealed” — φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible, brought into the open, as distinct from ἀποκαλύπτω, unveiling. ζωή, from the life family: the life of God Himself. καὶ ἑωράκαμεν καὶ μαρτυροῦμεν καὶ ἀπαγγέλλομεν, “and we have seen, and testify, and declare” — ὁράω, from the see family; μαρτυρέω, to bear witness, as in a court; ἀπαγγέλλω, to report. τὴν ζωὴν τὴν αἰώνιον, “the life, the eternal life” — αἰώνιος, from the time family: the life of the age to come, which was πρὸς τὸν πατέρα, ‘face to face with the Father’, the same phrase as John 1:1. μαρτυρέω is from the witness family — to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"},{"strongs":"G518","hebrew":"ἀπαγγέλλω","translit":"apangéllō","english":"declare"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":3,"text":"that which we have seen and heard we declare to you, that you also may have fellowship with us. Yes, and our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ.","understanding":"ὃ ἑωράκαμεν καὶ ἀκηκόαμεν ἀπαγγέλλομεν καὶ ὑμῖν, “that which we have seen and heard we declare to you” — ὁράω, from the see family; the apostles pass on what they received. ἵνα καὶ ὑμεῖς κοινωνίαν ἔχητε μεθ᾽ ἡμῶν, “that you also may have fellowship with us” — κοινωνία, partnership, sharing a common life. καὶ ἡ κοινωνία δὲ ἡ ἡμετέρα μετὰ τοῦ πατρὸς καὶ μετὰ τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “Yes, and our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ.” Fellowship with the apostles is fellowship with God.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G518","hebrew":"ἀπαγγέλλω","translit":"apangéllō","english":"declare"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"fellowship"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"}]},{"v":4,"text":"And we write these things to you, that our joy may be fulfilled.","understanding":"καὶ ταῦτα γράφομεν ὑμῖν, “And we write these things to you” — γράφω, the first of many ‘I write’ statements of purpose. ἵνα ἡ χαρὰ ἡμῶν ᾖ πεπληρωμένη, “that our joy may be fulfilled” — χαρά, joy; πληρόω, from the perfect family: filled to the brim, as Jesus prayed (John 15:11). Many manuscripts read ‘your joy’. Shared fellowship makes joy complete.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"fulfilled"}]},{"v":5,"text":"This is the message which we have heard from him and announce to you, that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all.","understanding":"καὶ ἔστιν αὕτη ἡ ἀγγελία, “This is the message” — ἀγγελία, the announcement. “which we have heard from him and announce to you” — ἀναγγέλλω, to declare fully. ὅτι ὁ θεὸς φῶς ἐστιν, “that God is light” — φῶς: not merely ‘God gives light’ but ‘God is light’: pure, true, holy, self-revealing. καὶ σκοτία ἐν αὐτῷ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδεμία, “and in him is no darkness at all” — σκοτία, from the darkness family: the state of darkness, spiritual blindness, John's favourite word for it; the double negative in Greek makes it emphatic: not even one shadow.","words":[{"strongs":"G31","hebrew":"ἀγγελία","translit":"angelía","english":"message"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G312","hebrew":"ἀναγγέλλω","translit":"anangéllō","english":"announce"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G4653","hebrew":"σκοτία","translit":"skotía","english":"darkness"}]},{"v":6,"text":"If we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in the darkness, we lie, and don’t tell the truth.","understanding":"ἐὰν εἴπωμεν ὅτι κοινωνίαν ἔχομεν μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “If we say that we have fellowship with him” — the first of three ‘if we say’ claims (vv. 6, 8, 10), probably the slogans of the false teachers. καὶ ἐν τῷ σκότει περιπατῶμεν, “and walk in the darkness” — σκότος, the second word of the darkness family: darkness as a realm, a way of life, as distinct from σκοτία in verse 5; περιπατέω, to walk, to live day by day. ψευδόμεθα καὶ οὐ ποιοῦμεν τὴν ἀλήθειαν, “we lie, and don’t tell the truth” — literally ‘do not do the truth’: truth is something you practise.","words":[{"strongs":"G2036","hebrew":"ἔπω","translit":"épō","english":"say"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"fellowship"},{"strongs":"G4655","hebrew":"σκότος","translit":"skótos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"tell"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin.","understanding":"ἐὰν δὲ ἐν τῷ φωτὶ περιπατῶμεν ὡς αὐτός ἐστιν ἐν τῷ φωτί, “But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light” — not ‘if we are sinless’, but if we live open and honest before God. κοινωνίαν ἔχομεν μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων, “we have fellowship with one another” — walking in the light joins believers together. καὶ τὸ αἷμα Ἰησοῦ τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ καθαρίζει ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ πάσης ἁμαρτίας, “and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” — καθαρίζω, present tense: keeps on cleansing; αἷμα, the real blood of a real body, against those who denied He came in the flesh; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G2842","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","english":"fellowship"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleanses"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":8,"text":"If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us.","understanding":"ἐὰν εἴπωμεν ὅτι ἁμαρτίαν οὐκ ἔχομεν, “If we say that we have no sin” — the second false claim: that we have no sinful nature, no sin principle. ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. ἑαυτοὺς πλανῶμεν, “we deceive ourselves” — πλανάω, to lead astray: self-deception. καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν ἡμῖν, “and the truth is not in us.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2036","hebrew":"ἔπω","translit":"épō","english":"say"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceive"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":9,"text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.","understanding":"ἐὰν ὁμολογῶμεν τὰς ἁμαρτίας ἡμῶν, “If we confess our sins” — ὁμολογέω, literally ‘to say the same thing’: to agree with God about our sin, specifically and honestly. πιστός ἐστιν καὶ δίκαιος, “he is faithful and righteous” — faithful to His promise, righteous because the price has been paid. ἵνα ἀφῇ ἡμῖν τὰς ἁμαρτίας, “to forgive us the sins” — ἀφίημι, to release, to send away. καὶ καθαρίσῃ ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ πάσης ἀδικίας, “and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” — ἀδικία, from the sin family: wrongness, injustice, as distinct from ἁμαρτία, missing the mark. God both forgives the debt and cleans the stain.","words":[{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confess"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"forgive"},{"strongs":"G2511","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","english":"cleanse"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"}]},{"v":10,"text":"If we say that we haven’t sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us.","understanding":"ἐὰν εἴπωμεν ὅτι οὐχ ἡμαρτήκαμεν, “If we say that we haven’t sinned” — the third false claim: denying that we have actually committed sins. ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family: to miss the mark. ψεύστην ποιοῦμεν αὐτόν, “we make him a liar” — because God says all have sinned (Romans 3:23). καὶ ὁ λόγος αὐτοῦ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν ἡμῖν, “and his word is not in us” — λόγος, from the word family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2036","hebrew":"ἔπω","translit":"épō","english":"say"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinned"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liar"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Hands That Touched the Word","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture an old man, his hair white, his voice soft, sitting with a group of young believers. He holds up his wrinkled hands and says, ‘These hands touched Him. These eyes saw Him walking by the lake. These ears heard Him speak. I leaned against His chest at supper. I saw His wounds after He rose.’ The room is silent.","meaning":"“That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life” (v. 1). Christianity is not built on ideas or feelings but on a Person who was heard, seen and touched. And the purpose is fellowship: “that you also may have fellowship with us. Yes, and our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ” (v. 3)."},{"title":"Light With No Shadow","verseRef":"v. 5","image":"Picture the sun at noon in a cloudless desert sky — so bright you cannot look at it, so pure that every shadow on the ground is sharp and dark. Now picture a light even purer, with no flicker, no dimming, no smoke, no shadow anywhere in it.","meaning":"“This is the message which we have heard from him and announce to you, that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (v. 5). God is perfectly pure, perfectly true, perfectly holy. There is nothing hidden, nothing false, nothing evil in Him. That is wonderful news — and searching news, because to come near Him is to be seen completely."},{"title":"Walking Out of the Shadows","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture two people at night. One hides in an alley, avoiding the street lamps, keeping secrets. The other walks down the middle of a lit street with friends on either side. She trips and gets mud on her clothes, but because she is in the light, she sees it at once and a friend helps her wash it off.","meaning":"“If we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in the darkness, we lie” (v. 6). “But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (v. 7). Walking in the light does not mean never getting dirty; it means living honestly before God, where the blood of Jesus keeps cleansing."},{"title":"Agreeing with God","verseRef":"v. 8-10","image":"Picture a patient in a doctor's office, insisting he is perfectly healthy while the X-ray on the wall shows a broken bone. The doctor cannot treat him until he admits what the X-ray shows. The moment he says, ‘Yes, it's broken,’ the healing can begin.","meaning":"“If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us” (v. 8). “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” (v. 9). Confession is simply agreeing with God's diagnosis. The cure is already paid for; it is waiting for honesty."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Word of Life (v. 1-4)","body":"The apostles proclaim what they heard, saw, looked at and touched — the eternal Life who was with the Father and was revealed — so that readers may share their fellowship with the Father and the Son, and so that joy may be complete."},{"label":"God Is Light (v. 5)","body":"The message received from Christ: God is light, with no darkness in Him at all."},{"label":"Three False Claims and God's Remedy (v. 6-10)","body":"Claiming fellowship with God while walking in darkness is a lie, but walking in the light brings fellowship and continual cleansing by Jesus' blood; claiming to have no sin is self-deception, but confessing sins brings God's faithful forgiveness and cleansing; claiming never to have sinned makes God a liar."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Heard, Seen, Touched","paragraphs":["John's letter opens without a greeting, plunging straight into the reality of Christ. “That which was from the beginning” (v. 1) echoes the first line of his Gospel: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” (John 1:1). Then John piles up the senses: heard, seen with our eyes, gazed upon, touched with our hands. The eternal Word became something you could hear and see and handle.","This matters because the false teachers in John's churches seem to have denied that the divine Christ truly came in a physical body. They may have taught that the spiritual Christ only seemed human, or came upon Jesus at His baptism and left before the cross. John answers with eyewitness testimony. He had touched the risen Lord, as Jesus invited the disciples: “See my hands and my feet, that it is truly me. Touch me and see, for a spirit doesn’t have flesh and bones, as you see that I have” (Luke 24:39). He had seen Thomas told, “Reach here your finger, and see my hands” (John 20:27).","The purpose of this testimony is fellowship and joy. “that which we have seen and heard we declare to you, that you also may have fellowship with us. Yes, and our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ” (v. 3). κοινωνία means sharing a common life. Christianity is not merely believing facts about Jesus but sharing life with God and with His people. Jesus prayed for this: “that they may all be one; even as you, Father, are in me, and I in you, that they also may be one in us” (John 17:21)."]},{"heading":"God Is Light","paragraphs":["The central message is simple and vast: “God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (v. 5). In Scripture light stands for truth, holiness and revelation; darkness for falsehood, sin and ignorance. Jesus said, “I am the light of the world. He who follows me will not walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life” (John 8:12). John's Gospel says, “In him was life, and the life was the light of men” (John 1:4).","That truth has a sharp edge. If God is pure light, then no one can claim to know Him while living comfortably in moral darkness. Jesus said, “This is the judgment, that the light has come into the world, and men loved the darkness rather than the light; for their works were evil” (John 3:19). The false teachers apparently claimed a special closeness to God while being careless about sin. John says that is a lie (v. 6).","But the light is also good news. The opposite of walking in darkness is not walking in perfection but walking in the light — living openly and honestly before God. “But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (v. 7). The present tense matters: the blood keeps on cleansing. In the light, sin is seen, confessed and washed away; in the dark, it is hidden and grows."]},{"heading":"Three Lies and One Remedy","paragraphs":["John lists three claims, each beginning ‘If we say’. The first: “If we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in the darkness, we lie, and don’t tell the truth” (v. 6) — claiming relationship with God while ignoring holiness. The second: “If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us” (v. 8) — denying that we have a sinful nature. The third: “If we say that we haven’t sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us” (v. 10) — denying that we have actually sinned. Each claim is a different way of avoiding the light.","Between them stands the remedy: “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” (v. 9). To confess (ὁμολογέω) is to ‘say the same’ as God about our sin — to call it what He calls it, specifically and honestly. God's response rests on His character: He is faithful to His promise and righteous because Christ's blood has paid the price. He forgives (releases the debt) and cleanses (removes the stain).","This verse was written to believers. Christians still sin; the question is what they do with it. The tax collector prayed, “God, be merciful to me, a sinner!” (Luke 18:13), and went home justified. Peter, when he first saw who Jesus was, fell at His knees: “Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, Lord” (Luke 5:8). Honest confession is not the enemy of assurance; it is the path to it."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Word Made Flesh and the Light of the World","paragraphs":["John's letter and his Gospel open in parallel. The Gospel: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” (John 1:1), and “The Word became flesh, and lived among us. We saw his glory, such glory as of the one and only Son of the Father, full of grace and truth” (John 1:14). The letter: what was from the beginning was heard, seen and touched. Both insist that the eternal Son truly took on flesh.","The cleansing blood of verse 7 is explained in Hebrews: “According to the law, nearly everything is cleansed with blood, and apart from shedding of blood there is no remission” (Hebrews 9:22), and “how much more will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself without defect to God, cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?” (Hebrews 9:14). James adds the communal side of confession: “Confess your offenses to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed” (James 5:16).","And the light that God is will one day fill everything: “The city has no need for the sun, neither of the moon, to shine, for the very glory of God illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb” (Revelation 21:23). Those who walk in the light now are walking towards that city. Jesus defined the life John speaks of: “This is eternal life, that they should know you, the only true God, and him whom you sent, Jesus Christ” (John 17:3)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Saw / looked upon","hebrew":"θεάομαι","translit":"theáomai","strongs":"G2300 (seen ὁράω, horáō, G3708; touched ψηλαφάω, psēlapháō, G5584)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “to look closely at, i.e. (by implication) perceive (literally or figuratively); by extension to visit”. John uses two words from the see family in verse 1. ὁράω is to see and perceive; θεάομαι is to gaze, to contemplate attentively over time — the root of ‘theatre’. Then ψηλαφάω: to handle, to verify by touch. John is not describing a vision or a feeling, but long, close, physical acquaintance with Jesus. The Word of life could be watched and handled."},{"english":"Fellowship","hebrew":"κοινωνία","translit":"koinōnía","strongs":"G2842","verses":"v. 3, 6, 7","insight":"Strong's: “partnership, i.e. (literally) participation, or (social) intercourse, or (pecuniary) benefaction.” κοινωνία comes from κοινός, ‘common’: having something in common, sharing in it. It was used of business partners and of marriage. In 1 John it means sharing the life of God with the Father, the Son and one another. It is not a social club but a shared life, and it depends on walking in the light. Sin breaks fellowship; confession restores it."},{"english":"Darkness","hebrew":"σκοτία","translit":"skotía","strongs":"G4653 (darkness σκότος, skótos, G4655)","verses":"v. 5, 6","insight":"Strong's: “dimness, obscurity (literally or figuratively).” John uses σκοτία in verse 5 and σκότος in verse 6 — two members of the darkness family. σκοτία is the state of darkness, absence of light; σκότος is darkness as a realm in which one can walk. In God there is not one particle of σκοτία; so those who live in σκότος cannot share His life. This study gathers them, with ζόφος and σκοτεινός, into a new darkness family."},{"english":"Confess","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","strongs":"G3670","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “to assent, i.e. covenant, acknowledge.” ὁμολογέω is made of ὁμοῦ, ‘together’, and λέγω, ‘to say’: to say the same thing. To confess sin is to say about it what God says — to stop excusing, minimising or renaming it. The same word is used for confessing Jesus as Lord (4:2, 15): saying about Him what God says. Confession is agreement with God, and agreement opens the door to forgiveness and cleansing."},{"english":"Cleanse","hebrew":"καθαρίζω","translit":"katharízō","strongs":"G2511 (forgive ἀφίημι, aphíēmi, G863)","verses":"v. 7, 9","insight":"Strong's: “to cleanse (literally or figuratively).” καθαρίζω is the word for ritual and moral cleansing, the root of ‘catharsis’. John uses it twice: the blood of Jesus cleanses (v. 7), and God cleanses from all unrighteousness when we confess (v. 9). Alongside it stands ἀφίημι, ‘to release, send away’: forgiveness cancels the debt; cleansing removes the stain. Both are complete — ‘all sin’, ‘all unrighteousness’."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is calling you out of hiding — into fellowship with God, honest confession and the cleansing of Jesus' blood.","items":[{"title":"Real Fellowship","body":"John wrote so that you could share in the fellowship of the Father and the Son (v. 3). Is your faith a living relationship, or only a set of beliefs? The Spirit invites you into real fellowship — time with God, honesty before Him, and shared life with His people. Where has your relationship with God become cold or formal?"},{"title":"Come Into the Light","body":"Is there an area of your life you keep in the dark — a habit, a secret, a resentment, a relationship? “If we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in the darkness, we lie” (v. 6). The Spirit does not expose you to shame you, but to cleanse you. Bring it into the light today."},{"title":"Agree With God","body":"Have you been minimising, excusing or renaming a sin? Calling it a weakness, a mistake, a personality trait? The Spirit asks you to say the same thing God says about it. “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” (v. 9). Name it specifically, and receive His forgiveness."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Fellowship Check","body":"Are you sharing real life with other believers, or only attending meetings (v. 3)? Who knows you well enough to see your darkness?"},{"title":"The Light Check","body":"Is there anything you would be embarrassed for God or your church to see (v. 6)? What are you hiding?"},{"title":"The Confession Check","body":"When did you last confess a specific sin to God by name (v. 9)? Do you tend to deny, excuse or minimise your sin (vv. 8, 10)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Daily Walk in the Light","body":"Each evening this week, spend five minutes reviewing the day before God. Ask the Spirit to show you where you walked in darkness — words, thoughts, actions, omissions. Confess each one specifically, agreeing with God about it. Then read verse 9 aloud and thank Him for His faithful forgiveness and cleansing."},{"title":"Step Into Fellowship","body":"Choose one trusted believer and share honestly with them about a struggle you have kept hidden. Pray together. Experience the truth of verse 7: walking in the light brings fellowship with one another and the cleansing of Jesus' blood."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You that the eternal Life who was with You was revealed — heard, seen and touched by eyewitnesses — and that through their testimony I can share in Your fellowship with Your Son, Jesus Christ. Make my joy complete in You. You are light, and in You is no darkness at all. I confess that I have sometimes claimed fellowship with You while walking in darkness. Forgive me. Bring every hidden thing into Your light. I do not want to deceive myself or make You a liar by denying my sin. I agree with You about my sins today, and I name them before You. Thank You that You are faithful and righteous to forgive me and to cleanse me from all unrighteousness. Thank You that the blood of Jesus Your Son keeps on cleansing me as I walk in the light. Help me to walk honestly with You and with my brothers and sisters. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Thomas Touches the Wounds","ref":"John 20:24-29","relatedVerses":"v. 1","summary":"Thomas was not with the disciples when Jesus first appeared after the resurrection, and he refused to believe unless he could touch the nail marks and put his hand into Jesus' side. A week later Jesus appeared again and said, “Reach here your finger, and see my hands. Reach here your hand, and put it into my side. Don’t be unbelieving, but believing” (John 20:27). Thomas answered, ‘My Lord and my God!’ John was in that room. When he writes of what ‘our hands touched’, he is speaking of a real, risen body."},{"title":"Peter at the Lake: ‘I Am a Sinful Man’","ref":"Luke 5:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 5-9","summary":"After a night of catching nothing, Peter let down his nets at Jesus' word and caught so many fish that the nets began to break. John and James were his partners in the other boat. Suddenly Peter saw who Jesus was, and “Simon Peter, when he saw it, fell down at Jesus’ knees, saying, ‘Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, Lord’” (Luke 5:8). In the light of Jesus' holiness he saw himself truly — and Jesus did not leave him, but called him. Confession in the light led not to rejection but to fellowship and mission."},{"title":"David Confesses His Sin","ref":"2 Samuel 12:1-13; Psalm 32; Psalm 51","relatedVerses":"v. 8-9","summary":"For about a year after his sin with Bathsheba and the murder of Uriah, David kept silent. In Psalm 32 he describes what it was like: his bones wasted away and his strength dried up. Then the prophet Nathan confronted him, and David said, ‘I have sinned against Yahweh.’ Nathan replied that God had put away his sin. In the psalm David writes, ‘I acknowledged my sin to you … and you forgave the iniquity of my sin.’ His story is 1 John 1:8–9 lived out: hiding sin brought misery; confessing it brought forgiveness."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 John","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Our Advocate, the New Commandment, and Remaining in Him","themeTitle":"“This Is How We Know That We Know Him: If We Keep His Commandments”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"My little children, I write these things to you so that you may not sin. If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous.","understanding":"τεκνία μου, “My little children” — τεκνίον, from the children family: a term of tender affection from an old pastor. ταῦτα γράφω ὑμῖν ἵνα μὴ ἁμάρτητε, “I write these things to you so that you may not sin” — ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family. Chapter 1 told them not to deny sin; now John tells them not to excuse it. καὶ ἐάν τις ἁμάρτῃ, παράκλητον ἔχομεν πρὸς τὸν πατέρα, “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father” — παράκλητος, one called alongside: a defence advocate in court; Jesus calls the Spirit ‘another Counselor’ (John 14:16). Ἰησοῦν Χριστὸν δίκαιον, “Jesus Christ, the righteous” — the only one qualified to plead our case.","words":[{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"little children"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G3875","hebrew":"παράκλητος","translit":"paráklētos","english":"Counselor"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"}]},{"v":2,"text":"And he is the atoning sacrifice for our sins, and not for ours only, but also for the whole world.","understanding":"καὶ αὐτὸς ἱλασμός ἐστιν περὶ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν, “And he is the atoning sacrifice for our sins” — ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; ἱλασμός, from the offering family: the sacrifice that satisfies God's justice and turns away His wrath. Jesus is both Advocate and sacrifice: He pleads His own blood. οὐ περὶ τῶν ἡμετέρων δὲ μόνον ἀλλὰ καὶ περὶ ὅλου τοῦ κόσμου, “and not for ours only, but also for the whole world” — κόσμος, from the world family: the whole of humanity, not only John's readers or only Jews. How this relates to who is finally saved is debated among Christians; the verse plainly declares the sacrifice sufficient and offered for all.","words":[{"strongs":"G2434","hebrew":"ἱλασμός","translit":"hilasmós","english":"atoning sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G3650","hebrew":"ὅλος","translit":"hólos","english":"whole"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":3,"text":"This is how we know that we know him: if we keep his commandments.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκομεν ὅτι ἐγνώκαμεν αὐτόν, “This is how we know that we know him” — γινώσκω twice, from the know family: knowing by experience, as distinct from οἶδα (vv. 20–21, 29), knowing as settled fact. The perfect tense ἐγνώκαμεν means a knowledge that has come and continues. ἐὰν τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ τηρῶμεν, “if we keep his commandments” — τηρέω, from the hold family: to guard carefully, to treasure. Obedience is the evidence of relationship (John 14:15).","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"}]},{"v":4,"text":"One who says, “I know him,” and doesn’t keep his commandments, is a liar, and the truth isn’t in him.","understanding":"ὁ λέγων ὅτι Ἔγνωκα αὐτόν, “One who says, ‘I know him,’” — another slogan of the false teachers, who boasted of knowledge. γινώσκω, from the know family. καὶ τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ μὴ τηρῶν, “and doesn’t keep his commandments” — τηρέω, from the hold family. ψεύστης ἐστίν, “is a liar, and the truth isn’t in him.” Knowledge without obedience is self-deception.","words":[{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"says"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liar"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":5,"text":"But whoever keeps his word, God’s love has most certainly been perfected in him. This is how we know that we are in him:","understanding":"ὃς δ᾽ ἂν τηρῇ αὐτοῦ τὸν λόγον, “But whoever keeps his word” — τηρέω, from the hold family; λόγος, from the word family: the whole of what He has said. ἀληθῶς ἐν τούτῳ ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ θεοῦ τετελείωται, “God’s love has most certainly been perfected in him” — ἀγάπη, from the love family; τελειόω, from the perfect family: brought to its goal, matured. God's love reaches its purpose when it produces obedience. ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκομεν ὅτι ἐν αὐτῷ ἐσμεν, “This is how we know that we are in him” — γινώσκω again, from the know family.","words":[{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfected"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]},{"v":6,"text":"he who says he remains in him ought himself also to walk just like he walked.","understanding":"ὁ λέγων ἐν αὐτῷ μένειν, “he who says he remains in him” — μένω, to remain, abide, stay: John's great word, used over twenty times in this letter. ὀφείλει καθὼς ἐκεῖνος περιεπάτησεν καὶ αὐτὸς οὕτως περιπατεῖν, “ought himself also to walk just like he walked” — ὀφείλω, to owe, to be obligated; περιπατέω, to walk, to live day by day. The standard is not a rule but a Person (John 13:15).","words":[{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"says"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G3784","hebrew":"ὀφείλω","translit":"opheílō","english":"ought"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Brothers, I write no new commandment to you, but an old commandment which you had from the beginning. The old commandment is the word which you heard from the beginning.","understanding":"ἀδελφοί, “Brothers” — many manuscripts read ἀγαπητοί, ‘beloved’. οὐκ ἐντολὴν καινὴν γράφω ὑμῖν, “I write no new commandment to you” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind. ἀλλ᾽ ἐντολὴν παλαιάν ἣν εἴχετε ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς, “but an old commandment which you had from the beginning” — παλαιός, old, long-standing; ἀρχή, beginning: the command to love was part of the gospel they first heard. ἡ ἐντολὴ ἡ παλαιά ἐστιν ὁ λόγος ὃν ἠκούσατε, “The old commandment is the word which you heard from the beginning” — λόγος, from the word family.","words":[{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"Brothers"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G3820","hebrew":"παλαιός","translit":"palaiós","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Again, I write a new commandment to you, which is true in him and in you; because the darkness is passing away, and the true light already shines.","understanding":"πάλιν ἐντολὴν καινὴν γράφω ὑμῖν, “Again, I write a new commandment to you” — καινός, from the new family. The same command is old (they have always known it) and new (Jesus filled it with new meaning: ‘as I have loved you’, John 13:34). ὅ ἐστιν ἀληθὲς ἐν αὐτῷ καὶ ἐν ὑμῖν, “which is true in him and in you” — first lived out by Jesus, now by them. ὅτι ἡ σκοτία παράγεται καὶ τὸ φῶς τὸ ἀληθινὸν ἤδη φαίνει, “because the darkness is passing away, and the true light already shines” — σκοτία, from the darkness family; παράγω, to pass by, like a parade going past; ἀληθινός, genuine, real; φαίνω, from the appear family: to shine. The new age has dawned.","words":[{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G227","hebrew":"ἀληθής","translit":"alēthḗs","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G4653","hebrew":"σκοτία","translit":"skotía","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G3855","hebrew":"παράγω","translit":"parágō","english":"passing away"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shines"}]},{"v":9,"text":"He who says he is in the light and hates his brother, is in the darkness even until now.","understanding":"ὁ λέγων ἐν τῷ φωτὶ εἶναι καὶ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ μισῶν, “He who says he is in the light and hates his brother” — μισέω, to hate, to be hostile, or simply to refuse love. ἐν τῇ σκοτίᾳ ἐστὶν ἕως ἄρτι, “is in the darkness even until now” — σκοτία, from the darkness family. Spiritual claims are tested by how we treat people.","words":[{"strongs":"G3004","hebrew":"λέγω","translit":"légō","english":"says"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hates"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G4653","hebrew":"σκοτία","translit":"skotía","english":"darkness"}]},{"v":10,"text":"He who loves his brother remains in the light, and there is no occasion for stumbling in him.","understanding":"ὁ ἀγαπῶν τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ ἐν τῷ φωτὶ μένει, “He who loves his brother remains in the light” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; μένω, to remain. καὶ σκάνδαλον ἐν αὐτῷ οὐκ ἔστιν, “and there is no occasion for stumbling in him” — σκάνδαλον, the trigger of a trap, a stumbling block: love removes what trips up both the lover and others.","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G4625","hebrew":"σκάνδαλον","translit":"skándalon","english":"occasion for stumbling"}]},{"v":11,"text":"But he who hates his brother is in the darkness, and walks in the darkness, and doesn’t know where he is going, because the darkness has blinded his eyes.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ μισῶν τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ ἐν τῇ σκοτίᾳ ἐστίν, “But he who hates his brother is in the darkness” — σκοτία, from the darkness family, three times in this verse. “and walks in the darkness, and doesn’t know where he is going” — εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family): he cannot see. ὅτι ἡ σκοτία ἐτύφλωσεν τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς αὐτοῦ, “because the darkness has blinded his eyes” — τυφλόω, to blind. Hatred does not only keep you in the dark; it destroys your ability to see.","words":[{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hates"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G4653","hebrew":"σκοτία","translit":"skotía","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walks"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"going"},{"strongs":"G5186","hebrew":"τυφλόω","translit":"typhlóō","english":"blinded"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"}]},{"v":12,"text":"I write to you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name’s sake.","understanding":"γράφω ὑμῖν, τεκνία, “I write to you, little children” — τεκνίον, from the children family: all believers, as God's dear children. ὅτι ἀφέωνται ὑμῖν αἱ ἁμαρτίαι διὰ τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ, “because your sins are forgiven you for his name’s sake” — ἀφίημι, perfect tense: forgiven and staying forgiven; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; ὄνομα, His name, His person and work. Assurance begins with forgiveness.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"little children"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"forgiven"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name’s"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I write to you, fathers, because you know him who is from the beginning. I write to you, young men, because you have overcome the evil one. I write to you, little children, because you know the Father.","understanding":"“I write to you, fathers, because you know him who is from the beginning” — πατέρες, mature believers; γινώσκω, from the know family: a deep, long acquaintance with Christ. “I write to you, young men, because you have overcome the evil one” — νεανίσκος, young men in their strength; νικάω, to conquer; πονηρός, from the evil family: ‘the evil one’, the devil himself. γράφω ὑμῖν, παιδία, “I write to you, little children” — παιδίον, the second word of the children family: young ones still learning, as distinct from τεκνία in verse 12. “because you know the Father” — even the newest believer knows God as Father.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3495","hebrew":"νεανίσκος","translit":"neanískos","english":"young men"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil one"},{"strongs":"G3813","hebrew":"παιδίον","translit":"paidíon","english":"little children"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I have written to you, fathers, because you know him who is from the beginning. I have written to you, young men, because you are strong, and the word of God remains in you, and you have overcome the evil one.","understanding":"ἔγραψα ὑμῖν, πατέρες, “I have written to you, fathers” — John repeats with a change of tense. “because you know him who is from the beginning” — γινώσκω, from the know family. “I have written to you, young men, because you are strong” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family: having inherent strength, as distinct from δύναμις, ability in action. καὶ ὁ λόγος τοῦ θεοῦ ἐν ὑμῖν μένει, “and the word of God remains in you” — λόγος, from the word family: the source of their strength. καὶ νενικήκατε τὸν πονηρόν, “and you have overcome the evil one” — πονηρός, from the evil family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3495","hebrew":"νεανίσκος","translit":"neanískos","english":"young men"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"strong"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil one"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him.","understanding":"μὴ ἀγαπᾶτε τὸν κόσμον μηδὲ τὰ ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ, “Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; κόσμος, from the world family: human society organised in rebellion against God, not the earth itself or its people, whom God loves (John 3:16). ἐάν τις ἀγαπᾷ τὸν κόσμον, οὐκ ἔστιν ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ πατρὸς ἐν αὐτῷ, “If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him” — ἀγάπη, from the love family. James said the same (James 4:4).","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father’s"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s.","understanding":"ὅτι πᾶν τὸ ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ, “For all that is in the world” — κόσμος, from the world family. Three things: ἡ ἐπιθυμία τῆς σαρκός, “the lust of the flesh” — sinful desires of the body; ἡ ἐπιθυμία τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν, “the lust of the eyes” — craving what we see; ἡ ἀλαζονεία τοῦ βίου, “and the pride of life” — ἀλαζονεία, boastful arrogance, the word of James 4:16; βίος, from the life family: the means of living, possessions, status, as distinct from ζωή (v. 25), the life of God. The same three appear in Eden and in the wilderness temptations. “isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s.”","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lust"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G212","hebrew":"ἀλαζονεία","translit":"alazoneía","english":"pride"},{"strongs":"G979","hebrew":"βίος","translit":"bíos","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father’s"}]},{"v":17,"text":"The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ κόσμος παράγεται καὶ ἡ ἐπιθυμία αὐτοῦ, “The world is passing away with its lusts” — παράγω, the word of verse 8: the world is a parade already moving past; κόσμος, from the world family. ὁ δὲ ποιῶν τὸ θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ μένει εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα, “but he who does God’s will remains forever” — θέλημα, God's will; μένω, to remain; αἰών, in the time family and the world family: into the age, forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3855","hebrew":"παράγω","translit":"parágō","english":"passing away"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"does"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Little children, these are the end times, and as you heard that the Antichrist is coming, even now many antichrists have arisen. By this we know that it is the final hour.","understanding":"παιδία, ἐσχάτη ὥρα ἐστίν, “Little children, these are the end times” — παιδίον, from the children family; literally ‘it is the last hour’: ὥρα, from the time family: an hour, a decisive moment, as distinct from χρόνος, a span of time. Since Christ's coming, history has been in its final hour. καὶ καθὼς ἠκούσατε ὅτι ἀντίχριστος ἔρχεται, “and as you heard that the Antichrist is coming” — ἀντίχριστος, one who opposes Christ and takes His place; a word used in the Bible only by John. “even now many antichrists have arisen. By this we know that it is the final hour” — γινώσκω, from the know family; ὥρα again. The spirit of antichrist is already at work.","words":[{"strongs":"G3813","hebrew":"παιδίον","translit":"paidíon","english":"Little children"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"times"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G500","hebrew":"ἀντίχριστος","translit":"antíchristos","english":"Antichrist"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]},{"v":19,"text":"They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us; for if they had belonged to us, they would have continued with us. But they left, that they might be revealed that none of them belong to us.","understanding":"ἐξ ἡμῶν ἐξῆλθαν, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἦσαν ἐξ ἡμῶν, “They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us” — ἐξέρχομαι, to go out: the false teachers had left the church. “for if they had belonged to us, they would have continued with us” — μένω, the ‘remain’ of the letter, here rendered ‘continued’. “But they left, that they might be revealed that none of them belong to us” — φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible. Their departure exposed what they had always been.","words":[{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"went out"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"continued"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"}]},{"v":20,"text":"You have an anointing from the Holy One, and you all have knowledge.","understanding":"καὶ ὑμεῖς χρῖσμα ἔχετε ἀπὸ τοῦ ἁγίου, “You have an anointing from the Holy One” — χρῖσμα, the anointing oil, here the Holy Spirit Himself, given by Christ (the ‘Anointed One’). The false teachers claimed special enlightenment; John says every believer has the anointing. καὶ οἴδατε πάντες, “and you all have knowledge” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family, since its root means ‘to see’): knowledge as a settled possession. The WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘you all know’; others read ‘you know all things’. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G5545","hebrew":"χρῖσμα","translit":"chrîsma","english":"anointing"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy One"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"have knowledge"}]},{"v":21,"text":"I have not written to you because you don’t know the truth, but because you know it, and because no lie is of the truth.","understanding":"οὐκ ἔγραψα ὑμῖν ὅτι οὐκ οἴδατε τὴν ἀλήθειαν, “I have not written to you because you don’t know the truth” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family, since its root means ‘to see’). ἀλλ᾽ ὅτι οἴδατε αὐτήν, “but because you know it” — John writes to confirm, not to inform. καὶ ὅτι πᾶν ψεῦδος ἐκ τῆς ἀληθείας οὐκ ἔστιν, “and because no lie is of the truth” — ψεῦδος, a lie: truth and lies have different fathers (John 8:44).","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G5579","hebrew":"ψεῦδος","translit":"pseûdos","english":"lie"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Who is the liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ? This is the Antichrist, he who denies the Father and the Son.","understanding":"τίς ἐστιν ὁ ψεύστης εἰ μὴ ὁ ἀρνούμενος ὅτι Ἰησοῦς οὐκ ἔστιν ὁ Χριστός, “Who is the liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ?” — ἀρνέομαι, to deny, disown: the false teachers denied that the man Jesus was the divine Christ. οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ ἀντίχριστος, “This is the Antichrist, he who denies the Father and the Son.” — ἀντίχριστος; to deny the Son is to deny the Father who sent Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liar"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denies"},{"strongs":"G500","hebrew":"ἀντίχριστος","translit":"antíchristos","english":"Antichrist"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Whoever denies the Son, the same doesn’t have the Father. He who confesses the Son has the Father also.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ ἀρνούμενος τὸν υἱὸν οὐδὲ τὸν πατέρα ἔχει, “Whoever denies the Son, the same doesn’t have the Father” — no one can have God while rejecting Jesus. ὁ ὁμολογῶν τὸν υἱὸν καὶ τὸν πατέρα ἔχει, “He who confesses the Son has the Father also” — ὁμολογέω, to confess, to say the same: the confession of 1:9 now about Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denies"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confesses"}]},{"v":24,"text":"Therefore, as for you, let that remain in you which you heard from the beginning. If that which you heard from the beginning remains in you, you also will remain in the Son, and in the Father.","understanding":"ὑμεῖς ὃ ἠκούσατε ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς, ἐν ὑμῖν μενέτω, “Therefore, as for you, let that remain in you which you heard from the beginning” — ἀρχή, the beginning of their faith; μένω three times in this verse. The defence against new teaching is the original gospel. “If that which you heard from the beginning remains in you, you also will remain in the Son, and in the Father.” If the truth remains in you, you remain in God.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":25,"text":"This is the promise which he promised us, the eternal life.","understanding":"καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ ἐπαγγελία ἣν αὐτὸς ἐπηγγείλατο ἡμῖν, “This is the promise which he promised us” — ἐπαγγελία and ἐπαγγέλλομαι. τὴν ζωὴν τὴν αἰώνιον, “the eternal life” — ζωή, from the life family: the life of God, as distinct from βίος in verse 16, the possessions and status of this life; αἰώνιος, from the time family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promised"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":26,"text":"These things I have written to you concerning those who would lead you astray.","understanding":"ταῦτα ἔγραψα ὑμῖν περὶ τῶν πλανώντων ὑμᾶς, “These things I have written to you concerning those who would lead you astray” — πλανάω, to lead astray, to deceive, as in 1:8. The false teachers were still trying to draw believers away.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"lead you astray"}]},{"v":27,"text":"As for you, the anointing which you received from him remains in you, and you don’t need for anyone to teach you. But as his anointing teaches you concerning all things, and is true, and is no lie, and even as it taught you, you will remain in him.","understanding":"καὶ ὑμεῖς τὸ χρῖσμα ὃ ἐλάβετε ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ μένει ἐν ὑμῖν, “As for you, the anointing which you received from him remains in you” — χρῖσμα, the Spirit; μένω. καὶ οὐ χρείαν ἔχετε ἵνα τις διδάσκῃ ὑμᾶς, “and you don’t need for anyone to teach you” — not a rejection of all teachers (John himself is teaching them), but of the false teachers who claimed believers needed their special knowledge. The Spirit leads into all truth (John 16:13). διδάσκω, to teach; ἀληθής, true; ψεῦδος, a lie. “you will remain in him.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5545","hebrew":"χρῖσμα","translit":"chrîsma","english":"anointing"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teach"},{"strongs":"G227","hebrew":"ἀληθής","translit":"alēthḗs","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G5579","hebrew":"ψεῦδος","translit":"pseûdos","english":"lie"}]},{"v":28,"text":"Now, little children, remain in him, that when he appears, we may have boldness, and not be ashamed before him at his coming.","understanding":"καὶ νῦν, τεκνία, μένετε ἐν αὐτῷ, “Now, little children, remain in him” — τεκνίον, from the children family; μένω. ἵνα ἐὰν φανερωθῇ σχῶμεν παρρησίαν, “that when he appears, we may have boldness” — φανερόω, from the appear family: His visible return; παρρησία, openness, confidence, freedom of speech. καὶ μὴ αἰσχυνθῶμεν ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ ἐν τῇ παρουσίᾳ αὐτοῦ, “and not be ashamed before him at his coming” — αἰσχύνομαι, to shrink back in shame; παρουσία, His royal arrival, the word of 2 Peter 1:16 and 3:4.","words":[{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"little children"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"appears"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G153","hebrew":"αἰσχύνομαι","translit":"aischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"}]},{"v":29,"text":"If you know that he is righteous, you know that everyone who practices righteousness has been born of him.","understanding":"ἐὰν εἰδῆτε ὅτι δίκαιός ἐστιν, “If you know that he is righteous” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family, since its root means ‘to see’): knowing as settled fact. γινώσκετε ὅτι πᾶς ὁ ποιῶν τὴν δικαιοσύνην ἐξ αὐτοῦ γεγέννηται, “you know that everyone who practices righteousness has been born of him” — γινώσκω, also from the know family: recognise by experience. Two ‘know’ words side by side: because you know what God is like, you can recognise His children. γεννάω, from the birth family: perfect tense, has been born and remains so. Righteous living does not cause new birth; it shows it.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"practices"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Advocate in the Courtroom","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a courtroom. You stand in the dock, guilty; the evidence is overwhelming. The accuser lists every charge. Then a man rises to speak for you — the Judge's own Son. He does not deny the charges. He holds up His scarred hands and says, ‘I have paid for every one.’ The Judge nods. The case is closed.","meaning":"“If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous” (v. 1). “And he is the atoning sacrifice for our sins, and not for ours only, but also for the whole world” (v. 2). Jesus is both your defence lawyer and the payment for your guilt. When you sin, do not run from God; run to your Advocate."},{"title":"The Old Commandment Made New","verseRef":"v. 7-11","image":"Picture an old road map, its folds worn, that every traveller has carried for generations. Then picture the same road at dawn, lit by a sunrise so bright that every turn and stone is visible for the first time. The road has not changed; the light has.","meaning":"The command to love is old — it was in the law and in the gospel from the beginning. But in Jesus it became new: “Again, I write a new commandment to you, which is true in him and in you; because the darkness is passing away, and the true light already shines” (v. 8). Hatred keeps a person in darkness and blinds their eyes (v. 11). Love walks in the full light of the new day."},{"title":"The Passing Parade","verseRef":"v. 15-17","image":"Picture a glittering parade passing down a city street — floats of gold and silk, music, crowds cheering, celebrities waving. You are tempted to run and join it. But as you watch, you see that it is moving steadily away, around a corner, and beyond it the street is empty.","meaning":"“Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world” (v. 15). “For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s” (v. 16). “The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (v. 17). Do not give your heart to a parade that is already leaving."},{"title":"Standing Boldly at His Coming","verseRef":"v. 28","image":"Picture a soldier who has stayed at his post through a long night while others deserted. At dawn the King arrives. Some of the deserters hide their faces in shame; the faithful soldier steps forward to greet his King with joy and confidence.","meaning":"“Now, little children, remain in him, that when he appears, we may have boldness, and not be ashamed before him at his coming” (v. 28). The secret of confidence on that day is remaining in Christ now — staying in the truth you heard from the beginning, keeping His word and loving His people."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Our Advocate and Sacrifice (v. 1-2)","body":"John writes so that his children will not sin; but if anyone sins, Jesus Christ the Righteous is our Advocate with the Father and the atoning sacrifice for our sins and for the whole world."},{"label":"The Test of Obedience (v. 3-6)","body":"We know that we know Him if we keep His commandments; whoever claims to know Him but disobeys is a liar; in whoever keeps His word God's love is perfected; whoever claims to remain in Him must walk as Jesus walked."},{"label":"The Test of Love (v. 7-11)","body":"The command to love is old, yet new, because the darkness is passing and the true light is shining; whoever hates his brother is still in darkness and blinded, but whoever loves his brother remains in the light."},{"label":"Assurance for Every Stage (v. 12-14)","body":"John writes to little children whose sins are forgiven and who know the Father, to fathers who know Him who is from the beginning, and to young men who are strong, have God's word remaining in them and have overcome the evil one."},{"label":"Do Not Love the World (v. 15-17)","body":"Love for the world — the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes and the pride of life — excludes the Father's love; the world is passing away, but whoever does God's will remains forever."},{"label":"Antichrists and the Anointing (v. 18-29)","body":"It is the last hour and many antichrists have arisen, who went out from the church because they never belonged; believers have the anointing of the Holy One and know the truth; whoever denies the Son does not have the Father; let what they heard from the beginning remain in them, and remain in Christ so as to be confident at His coming; everyone who practises righteousness has been born of Him."}],"sections":[{"heading":"When We Sin: Our Advocate","paragraphs":["Chapter 1 warned against denying sin. Chapter 2 begins by warning against excusing it: “My little children, I write these things to you so that you may not sin” (v. 1). Grace is never a licence. But John immediately adds comfort: “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous.” The word παράκλητος describes a defence advocate who stands beside the accused. Jesus pleads for His people — not by denying their guilt, but by presenting His own sacrifice. Hebrews says, “he lives forever to make intercession for them” (Hebrews 7:25), and Paul asks, “Who is he who condemns? It is Christ who died, yes rather, who was raised from the dead, who is at the right hand of God, who also makes intercession for us” (Romans 8:34).","That sacrifice is described with the word ἱλασμός: “And he is the atoning sacrifice for our sins” (v. 2). It means a sacrifice that satisfies God's justice and turns away His holy wrath against sin — not by changing God's mind, but by God Himself providing the sacrifice. Paul uses a related word: God sent Christ “to be an atoning sacrifice, through faith in his blood” (Romans 3:25).","And its reach is universal: “not for ours only, but also for the whole world” (v. 2). John the Baptist called Jesus “the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29). Christians understand this differently in relation to who is finally saved; what the verse clearly declares is that Christ's sacrifice is sufficient for all, offered to all, and that there is no other."]},{"heading":"How We Know That We Know Him","paragraphs":["The false teachers claimed to know God in a special way. John gives the test: “This is how we know that we know him: if we keep his commandments” (v. 3). Jesus said the same: “If you love me, keep my commandments” (John 14:15). Obedience is not the root of salvation but its fruit. A person who says ‘I know him’ while ignoring His commands “is a liar, and the truth isn’t in him” (v. 4).","Then John defines the standard: “he who says he remains in him ought himself also to walk just like he walked” (v. 6). The Christian life is not measured by a list but by a life: Jesus' own. And the command that sums up that life is love. It is old, because it was there from the beginning of their faith (v. 7); it is new, because Jesus filled it with fresh meaning — “A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another” (John 13:34) — and because the new age has dawned: “the darkness is passing away, and the true light already shines” (v. 8).","So love becomes the test of light. “He who says he is in the light and hates his brother, is in the darkness even until now” (v. 9). Hatred is not only wrong; it is blinding: “the darkness has blinded his eyes” (v. 11). You cannot see clearly — spiritually or relationally — while you nurse hatred. But “He who loves his brother remains in the light, and there is no occasion for stumbling in him” (v. 10)."]},{"heading":"Do Not Love the World","paragraphs":["After assuring every group in the church — children, fathers and young men — that their sins are forgiven, that they know God and have overcome the evil one (vv. 12–14), John warns them: “Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him” (v. 15). The ‘world’ here is not creation or people, whom God loves, but the whole system of values and desires that leaves God out. Jesus said, “No one can serve two masters” (Matthew 6:24), and James, “friendship with the world is hostility toward God” (James 4:4).","John names the world's three appeals: “the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life” (v. 16). They are the same three that worked in Eden, when the fruit was good for food, a delight to the eyes and desirable to make one wise; and in the wilderness, where Jesus was tempted to satisfy His hunger, to seize the kingdoms He was shown, and to prove Himself by a spectacular leap (Matthew 4:1–11). Where Adam fell, Christ stood.","John's argument is simple: the world does not last. “The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (v. 17). To love what is passing is to invest in what will be lost. To do God's will is to share in what remains forever."]},{"heading":"Antichrists, the Anointing and Remaining","paragraphs":["“Little children, these are the end times” — literally ‘it is the last hour’ (v. 18). The church had been taught that the Antichrist would come, and Paul had described ‘the man of sin’ (2 Thessalonians 2:3). John says the spirit of antichrist is already at work: “even now many antichrists have arisen.” They are those who deny “that Jesus is the Christ” (v. 22) — who deny that the man Jesus is the eternal Son of God come in the flesh. And they came from inside the church: “They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us; for if they had belonged to us, they would have continued with us” (v. 19).","How are believers to stand? Not by seeking a new, secret knowledge, but by the anointing every believer already has: “You have an anointing from the Holy One, and you all have knowledge” (v. 20). The Holy Spirit is the Spirit of truth who leads into all truth (John 16:13). And by holding to the original gospel: “Therefore, as for you, let that remain in you which you heard from the beginning” (v. 24). New is not always better; in theology it is often worse.","The chapter ends with a call and a sign. The call: “Now, little children, remain in him, that when he appears, we may have boldness, and not be ashamed before him at his coming” (v. 28). The sign: “If you know that he is righteous, you know that everyone who practices righteousness has been born of him” (v. 29). Right living does not create new birth; it reveals it. The family likeness shows."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Advocate, the New Commandment and the Overcomers","paragraphs":["The Advocate (παράκλητος) of verse 1 is Jesus; in John's Gospel Jesus promises ‘another’ Advocate: “I will pray to the Father, and he will give you another Counselor, that he may be with you forever” (John 14:16). So believers have an Advocate in heaven (the Son) and an Advocate within (the Spirit) — the ‘anointing’ of verses 20 and 27, of whom Jesus said, “he will teach you all things” (John 14:26).","Keeping His commandments is the language of the upper room: “One who has my commandments, and keeps them, that person is one who loves me” (John 14:21), and “If you keep my commandments, you will remain in my love; even as I have kept my Father’s commandments, and remain in his love” (John 15:10). John is recalling what he heard at the supper where he leaned on Jesus' breast.","The young men who have overcome the evil one share the victory of their Lord, who said, “In the world you have oppression; but cheer up! I have overcome the world” (John 16:33). Revelation describes such overcomers: “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony” (Revelation 12:11). And the shame John warns against at Christ's coming echoes Jesus' own words: “For whoever will be ashamed of me and of my words in this adulterous and sinful generation, the Son of Man also will be ashamed of him” (Mark 8:38)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Counselor / Advocate","hebrew":"παράκλητος","translit":"paráklētos","strongs":"G3875","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “an intercessor, consoler.” παράκλητος, from παρά (beside) and καλέω (call), is one called alongside to help — especially a defence advocate in court. In John's Gospel it is used four times of the Holy Spirit; here it is used of Jesus, who stands before the Father on behalf of sinning believers. He does not plead their innocence; He pleads His own sacrifice. When you sin, the One who speaks for you is righteous, and He has already paid."},{"english":"Atoning sacrifice","hebrew":"ἱλασμός","translit":"hilasmós","strongs":"G2434","verses":"v. 2; 4:10","insight":"Strong's: “atonement, i.e. (concretely) an expiator.” ἱλασμός is the sacrifice that removes guilt and turns away God's righteous anger against sin. In the Greek Old Testament related words describe the mercy seat and the Day of Atonement. This study adds it to the offering family. John uses it twice: here of Christ's sacrifice for the whole world, and in 4:10 as the supreme proof of God's love. God Himself provided the sacrifice His justice required."},{"english":"Remain / abide","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","strongs":"G3306","verses":"v. 6, 10, 14, 17, 19, 24, 27, 28","insight":"Strong's: “to stay (in a given place, state, relation or expectancy).” μένω is John's signature word, used over twenty times in this letter and often in his Gospel (‘Remain in me, and I in you’, John 15:4). The WEB translates it ‘remain’, ‘continue’ (v. 19) and elsewhere ‘abide’ — but it is one Greek word. It describes a settled, ongoing relationship: Christ remaining in the believer and the believer in Christ, the word remaining in them, love remaining. The false teachers left; true believers remain."},{"english":"Antichrist","hebrew":"ἀντίχριστος","translit":"antíchristos","strongs":"G500","verses":"v. 18, 22; 4:3","insight":"Strong's: “an opponent of the Messiah.” ἀντίχριστος is found in the Bible only in John's letters. The prefix ἀντί can mean ‘against’ or ‘instead of’: an antichrist opposes Christ and puts something in His place. John knows the church expects a final Antichrist, but says that many antichrists have already come — anyone who denies that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh. The spirit of antichrist is not only future; it is present wherever Christ's person is denied."},{"english":"Anointing","hebrew":"χρῖσμα","translit":"chrîsma","strongs":"G5545 (Holy One ἅγιος, hágios, G40)","verses":"v. 20, 27","insight":"Strong's: “an unguent or smearing, i.e. (figuratively) the special endowment (\"chrism\") of the Holy Spirit”. χρῖσμα comes from χρίω, to anoint, the root of Χριστός, ‘the Anointed One’. Every believer shares in Christ's anointing by receiving the Holy Spirit. The false teachers claimed an elite knowledge; John insists that all believers have the anointing and therefore all know the truth. The Spirit does not replace teachers, but He enables every believer to recognise the truth and reject the lie."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is testing your knowledge of God by your obedience and your love — and calling you to remain in Christ.","items":[{"title":"Run to Your Advocate","body":"When you sin, do you run away from God in shame, or run to Him because you have an Advocate? “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father, Jesus Christ, the righteous” (v. 1). The Spirit wants you to stop hiding and come straight to the Father, trusting the One who pleads His own blood for you."},{"title":"Who Are You Not Loving?","body":"“He who says he is in the light and hates his brother, is in the darkness even until now” (v. 9). Hatred is not always shouting; it can be coldness, avoidance, contempt or refusal to forgive. The Spirit brings a name to mind. Ask Him to show you how resentment has blinded your eyes (v. 11), and to give you love that walks in the light."},{"title":"What Is Pulling Your Heart?","body":"Is it the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes or the pride of life (v. 16)? Comfort, possessions, appearance, status, image? The Spirit reminds you that “The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (v. 17). Where is He asking you to let go of something that is passing?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Obedience Check","body":"Is there a command of Jesus you know but are not keeping (vv. 3–4)? Would someone watching your life say you walk as Jesus walked (v. 6)?"},{"title":"The Love Check","body":"Is there anyone in your church or family you are avoiding, resenting or treating with contempt (vv. 9–11)? What would love in the light look like toward them?"},{"title":"The World Check","body":"Which of the three — lust of the flesh, lust of the eyes, pride of life — pulls on you most (v. 16)? What are you investing in that is passing away?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Walk as He Walked","body":"Choose one Gospel story this week — Jesus washing feet, touching a leper, welcoming children, forgiving from the cross. Ask: ‘How did He walk here?’ Then find one way to walk like that in your own situation this week (v. 6). Write down what you did and what you learned."},{"title":"Test What You Hear","body":"This month, whenever you encounter a new teaching or popular idea about God, ask three questions from this chapter: Does it confess that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God come in the flesh (v. 22)? Does it agree with what was taught from the beginning (v. 24)? Does it lead to obedience and love (vv. 3–11)?"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You that when I sin I have an Advocate with You, Jesus Christ the Righteous, and that He is the atoning sacrifice for my sins and for the whole world. Keep me from sin, and when I fall, bring me straight to Him. Let my knowledge of You show in obedience. Help me keep Your word and walk as Jesus walked, so that Your love is made complete in me. Search my heart for hatred, coldness or contempt toward any brother or sister, and lead me out of that darkness into the light of love. Thank You that my sins are forgiven for Your name's sake, that I know You as Father, and that in Christ I can overcome the evil one. Guard me from loving the world — the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes and the pride of life — for it is passing away. Thank You for the anointing of Your Spirit, who teaches me the truth. Keep me in the truth I heard from the beginning, and keep me remaining in Christ, so that when He appears I may stand with confidence and not shrink back in shame. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Jesus Washes the Disciples' Feet","ref":"John 13:1-35","relatedVerses":"v. 6-8","summary":"On the night before He died, Jesus rose from supper, tied a towel around His waist, and washed His disciples' feet — including Peter's, and the feet of Judas who would betray Him. Then He said, “For I have given you an example, that you also should do as I have done to you” (John 13:15). That same night He gave the new commandment: “A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another” (John 13:34). John was there, leaning on Jesus' breast. Decades later he writes that whoever remains in Him must walk as He walked."},{"title":"Jesus Tempted in the Wilderness","ref":"Matthew 4:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 15-17","summary":"After forty days of fasting, Jesus was tempted three times. To turn stones into bread — the lust of the flesh. To gain all the kingdoms of the world and their glory — the lust of the eyes: “the devil took him to an exceedingly high mountain, and showed him all the kingdoms of the world, and their glory” (Matthew 4:8). To throw Himself from the temple to prove who He was — the pride of life. Each time He answered with God's word and did the Father's will. Where Adam and Eve fell to the same three appeals in Eden, Jesus overcame — and so can those who remain in Him."},{"title":"Judas Goes Out into the Night","ref":"John 13:21-30","relatedVerses":"v. 19","summary":"At the Last Supper, Jesus said one of the Twelve would betray Him. John, leaning against Jesus, asked who it was, and Jesus gave the bread to Judas. Then, John records, Judas “went out immediately. It was night.” Judas had walked with Jesus for three years, heard His teaching and seen His miracles, yet he did not belong. His going out revealed what he was. John writes of the false teachers in the same way: “They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us” (v. 19)."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 John","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Children of God and Love in Deed and Truth","themeTitle":"“See How Great a Love the Father Has Given to Us”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God! For this cause the world doesn’t know us, because it didn’t know him.","understanding":"ἴδετε ποταπὴν ἀγάπην δέδωκεν ἡμῖν ὁ πατήρ, “See how great a love the Father has given to us” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family): ‘Look!’; ποταπός, of what sort, from what country: a love so foreign to this world it astonishes; ἀγάπη, from the love family. ἵνα τέκνα θεοῦ κληθῶμεν, “that we should be called children of God!” — τέκνον, from the children family: children by birth, not merely by adoption of name. Many manuscripts add ‘and we are’. διὰ τοῦτο ὁ κόσμος οὐ γινώσκει ἡμᾶς, ὅτι οὐκ ἔγνω αὐτόν, “For this cause the world doesn’t know us, because it didn’t know him” — κόσμος, from the world family; γινώσκω, from the know family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"See"},{"strongs":"G4217","hebrew":"ποταπός","translit":"potapós","english":"how great"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Beloved, now we are children of God, and it is not yet revealed what we will be. But we know that, when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is.","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, νῦν τέκνα θεοῦ ἐσμεν, “Beloved, now we are children of God” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family; τέκνον, from the children family: the present is certain. καὶ οὔπω ἐφανερώθη τί ἐσόμεθα, “and it is not yet revealed what we will be” — φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible; the future is not yet visible. οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἐὰν φανερωθῇ, ὅμοιοι αὐτῷ ἐσόμεθα, “But we know that, when he is revealed, we will be like him” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: settled knowledge; ὅμοιος, alike. ὅτι ὀψόμεθα αὐτὸν καθώς ἐστιν, “for we will see him just as he is” — ὁράω, also from the see family. Seeing Him will transform us (2 Corinthians 3:18).","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Everyone who has this hope set on him purifies himself, even as he is pure.","understanding":"καὶ πᾶς ὁ ἔχων τὴν ἐλπίδα ταύτην ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ, “Everyone who has this hope set on him” — ἐλπίς, the confident expectation of seeing Christ. ἁγνίζει ἑαυτὸν καθὼς ἐκεῖνος ἁγνός ἐστιν, “purifies himself, even as he is pure” — ἁγνίζω, to make clean, the word of James 4:8 and 1 Peter 1:22; ἁγνός, pure. Hope is not passive; it is purifying.","words":[{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G48","hebrew":"ἁγνίζω","translit":"hagnízō","english":"purifies"},{"strongs":"G53","hebrew":"ἁγνός","translit":"hagnós","english":"pure"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Everyone who sins also commits lawlessness. Sin is lawlessness.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ ποιῶν τὴν ἁμαρτίαν καὶ τὴν ἀνομίαν ποιεῖ, “Everyone who sins also commits lawlessness” — two words from the sin family: ἁμαρτία, missing the mark, and ἀνομία, lawlessness, rebellion against God's law. καὶ ἡ ἁμαρτία ἐστὶν ἡ ἀνομία, “Sin is lawlessness.” — not a small slip but defiance; the false teachers may have treated sin as unimportant. ‘Lawlessness’ also recalls the ‘man of lawlessness’ of the last days (2 Thessalonians 2:3).","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"commits"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G458","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","english":"lawlessness"}]},{"v":5,"text":"You know that he was revealed to take away our sins, and in him is no sin.","understanding":"καὶ οἴδατε ὅτι ἐκεῖνος ἐφανερώθη ἵνα τὰς ἁμαρτίας ἄρῃ, “You know that he was revealed to take away our sins” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family); φανερόω, from the appear family: His first coming; αἴρω, to lift and carry away, the word of John 1:29; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. καὶ ἁμαρτία ἐν αὐτῷ οὐκ ἔστιν, “and in him is no sin” — the sinless One came to remove sin. To persist in sin is to oppose the purpose of His coming.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G142","hebrew":"αἴρω","translit":"aírō","english":"take away"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Whoever remains in him doesn’t sin. Whoever sins hasn’t seen him and doesn’t know him.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ ἐν αὐτῷ μένων οὐχ ἁμαρτάνει, “Whoever remains in him doesn’t sin” — μένω, to remain; ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family, in the present tense: does not keep on sinning as a settled way of life. John has already said that believers do sin and must confess (1:8–2:1); here he describes the direction of a life. πᾶς ὁ ἁμαρτάνων οὐχ ἑώρακεν αὐτὸν οὐδὲ ἔγνωκεν αὐτόν, “Whoever sins hasn’t seen him and doesn’t know him” — ὁράω, from the see family; γινώσκω, from the know family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Little children, let no one lead you astray. He who does righteousness is righteous, even as he is righteous.","understanding":"τεκνία, μηδεὶς πλανάτω ὑμᾶς, “Little children, let no one lead you astray” — τεκνίον, from the children family; πλανάω, the deceiving of 2:26. ὁ ποιῶν τὴν δικαιοσύνην δίκαιός ἐστιν, “He who does righteousness is righteous, even as he is righteous” — the false teachers apparently said you could be righteous without doing righteousness. John says practice reveals nature.","words":[{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"Little children"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"lead you astray"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"does"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"}]},{"v":8,"text":"He who sins is of the devil, for the devil has been sinning from the beginning. To this end the Son of God was revealed: that he might destroy the works of the devil.","understanding":"ὁ ποιῶν τὴν ἁμαρτίαν ἐκ τοῦ διαβόλου ἐστίν, “He who sins is of the devil” — ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; διάβολος, the slanderer. ὅτι ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς ὁ διάβολος ἁμαρτάνει, “for the devil has been sinning from the beginning” — ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family, present tense: continuous. εἰς τοῦτο ἐφανερώθη ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, “To this end the Son of God was revealed” — φανερόω, from the appear family. ἵνα λύσῃ τὰ ἔργα τοῦ διαβόλου, “that he might destroy the works of the devil” — λύω, to loose, to undo, to dismantle; ἔργον, from the work family. Jesus came to take apart everything the devil has built.","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinning"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"destroy"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Whoever is born of God doesn’t commit sin, because his seed remains in him; and he can’t sin, because he is born of God.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ γεγεννημένος ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἁμαρτίαν οὐ ποιεῖ, “Whoever is born of God doesn’t commit sin” — γεννάω, from the birth family: perfect tense, has been born and remains so; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. ὅτι σπέρμα αὐτοῦ ἐν αὐτῷ μένει, “because his seed remains in him” — σπέρμα, seed: God's own life, implanted by the Spirit and the word (1 Peter 1:23). καὶ οὐ δύναται ἁμαρτάνειν, “and he can’t sin” — δύναμαι, from the power family: is not able. Read with 1:8–10, this means that a child of God cannot go on sinning as a settled way of life, because God's nature in him resists it; it does not teach sinless perfection.","words":[{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"commit"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"seed"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"can’t"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"}]},{"v":10,"text":"In this the children of God are revealed, and the children of the devil. Whoever doesn’t do righteousness is not of God, neither is he who doesn’t love his brother.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ φανερά ἐστιν τὰ τέκνα τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τὰ τέκνα τοῦ διαβόλου, “In this the children of God are revealed, and the children of the devil” — φανερός, from the appear family: plain, out in the open; τέκνον, from the children family: family likeness shows. “Whoever doesn’t do righteousness is not of God, neither is he who doesn’t love his brother.” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family. Two marks: righteousness and love.","words":[{"strongs":"G5318","hebrew":"φανερός","translit":"phanerós","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For this is the message which you heard from the beginning, that we should love one another;","understanding":"ὅτι αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ ἀγγελία ἣν ἠκούσατε ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς, “For this is the message which you heard from the beginning” — ἀγγελία, the message, as in 1:5. ἵνα ἀγαπῶμεν ἀλλήλους, “that we should love one another” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family. The gospel always included love.","words":[{"strongs":"G31","hebrew":"ἀγγελία","translit":"angelía","english":"message"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"}]},{"v":12,"text":"unlike Cain, who was of the evil one, and killed his brother. Why did he kill him? Because his deeds were evil, and his brother’s righteous.","understanding":"οὐ καθὼς Κάϊν ἐκ τοῦ πονηροῦ ἦν, “unlike Cain, who was of the evil one” — πονηρός, from the evil family: actively harmful, ‘the evil one’, the devil. καὶ ἔσφαξεν τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ, “and killed his brother” — σφάζω, to slaughter, to butcher. “Why did he kill him? Because his deeds were evil, and his brother’s righteous.” — ἔργον, from the work family; πονηρός again. Cain hated Abel not for any wrong Abel did but because Abel's goodness exposed his evil.","words":[{"strongs":"G2535","hebrew":"Κάϊν","translit":"Káïn","english":"Cain"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil one"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Don’t be surprised, my brothers, if the world hates you.","understanding":"μὴ θαυμάζετε, ἀδελφοί, “Don’t be surprised, my brothers” — θαυμάζω, to marvel. εἰ μισεῖ ὑμᾶς ὁ κόσμος, “if the world hates you” — μισέω; κόσμος, from the world family. The Cain spirit is still alive; Jesus said, ‘If the world hates you, you know that it has hated me before it hated you’ (John 15:18).","words":[{"strongs":"G2296","hebrew":"θαυμάζω","translit":"thaumázō","english":"surprised"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hates"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":14,"text":"We know that we have passed out of death into life, because we love the brothers. He who doesn’t love his brother remains in death.","understanding":"ἡμεῖς οἴδαμεν ὅτι μεταβεβήκαμεν ἐκ τοῦ θανάτου εἰς τὴν ζωήν, “We know that we have passed out of death into life” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family); μεταβαίνω, to move house, to cross over, the word Jesus used in John 5:24; θάνατος, death; ζωή, from the life family: the life of God. ὅτι ἀγαπῶμεν τοὺς ἀδελφούς, “because we love the brothers” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family: love is the evidence, not the cause, of new life. “He who doesn’t love his brother remains in death.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3327","hebrew":"μεταβαίνω","translit":"metabaínō","english":"passed"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Whoever hates his brother is a murderer, and you know that no murderer has eternal life remaining in him.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ μισῶν τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ ἀνθρωποκτόνος ἐστίν, “Whoever hates his brother is a murderer” — ἀνθρωποκτόνος, a man-killer, the word Jesus used of the devil (John 8:44). Hatred is murder in the heart, as Jesus taught (Matthew 5:21–22). καὶ οἴδατε ὅτι πᾶς ἀνθρωποκτόνος οὐκ ἔχει ζωὴν αἰώνιον ἐν αὐτῷ μένουσαν, “and you know that no murderer has eternal life remaining in him” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family); ζωή, from the life family; αἰώνιος, from the time family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hates"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G443","hebrew":"ἀνθρωποκτόνος","translit":"anthrōpoktónos","english":"murderer"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remaining"}]},{"v":16,"text":"By this we know love, because he laid down his life for us. And we ought to lay down our lives for the brothers.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ ἐγνώκαμεν τὴν ἀγάπην, “By this we know love” — γινώσκω, from the know family; ἀγάπη, from the love family. ὅτι ἐκεῖνος ὑπὲρ ἡμῶν τὴν ψυχὴν αὐτοῦ ἔθηκεν, “because he laid down his life for us” — ψυχή, from the life family: His own self, His natural life, as distinct from ζωή (v. 14), the divine life He gives; τίθημι, to lay down, the shepherd's word (John 10:11). καὶ ἡμεῖς ὀφείλομεν ὑπὲρ τῶν ἀδελφῶν τὰς ψυχὰς θεῖναι, “And we ought to lay down our lives for the brothers” — ὀφείλω, to owe: the cross is not only our salvation but our pattern. The John 3:16 of love in action.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"laid down"},{"strongs":"G3784","hebrew":"ὀφείλω","translit":"opheílō","english":"ought"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":17,"text":"But whoever has the world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and closes his heart of compassion against him, how does God’s love remain in him?","understanding":"ὃς δ᾽ ἂν ἔχῃ τὸν βίον τοῦ κόσμου, “But whoever has the world’s goods” — βίος, the third word of the life family in this passage: the means of living, possessions, as distinct from ζωή (v. 14) and ψυχή (v. 16); κόσμος, from the world family. καὶ θεωρῇ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ χρείαν ἔχοντα, “and sees his brother in need” — θεωρέω, from the see family: to look at, to observe at length; χρεία, need. καὶ κλείσῃ τὰ σπλάγχνα αὐτοῦ ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “and closes his heart of compassion against him” — κλείω, to shut a door; σπλάγχνα, from the mercy family: the inner parts, where compassion is felt. “how does God’s love remain in him?” — ἀγάπη, from the love family. If you will not give your βίος, how can you claim you would give your ψυχή?","words":[{"strongs":"G979","hebrew":"βίος","translit":"bíos","english":"world’s goods"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world’s"},{"strongs":"G2334","hebrew":"θεωρέω","translit":"theōréō","english":"sees"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"closes"},{"strongs":"G4698","hebrew":"σπλάγχνον","translit":"splánchnon","english":"compassion"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"}]},{"v":18,"text":"My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth.","understanding":"τεκνία μου, “My little children” — τεκνίον, from the children family. μὴ ἀγαπῶμεν λόγῳ μηδὲ τῇ γλώσσῃ, “let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; λόγος, from the word family, here mere talk; γλῶσσα, the tongue of James 3. ἀλλὰ ἐν ἔργῳ καὶ ἀληθείᾳ, “but in deed and truth” — ἔργον, from the work family: the same truth James taught (James 2:15–16).","words":[{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"little children"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deed"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":19,"text":"And by this we know that we are of the truth, and persuade our hearts before him,","understanding":"καὶ ἐν τούτῳ γνωσόμεθα ὅτι ἐκ τῆς ἀληθείας ἐσμέν, “And by this we know that we are of the truth” — γινώσκω, from the know family: love in action is evidence of belonging to the truth. καὶ ἔμπροσθεν αὐτοῦ πείσομεν τὰς καρδίας ἡμῶν, “and persuade our hearts before him” — πείθω, to persuade, to reassure, to calm. A troubled conscience can be quieted by the evidence of real love.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"persuade"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"}]},{"v":20,"text":"because if our heart condemns us, God is greater than our heart, and knows all things.","understanding":"ὅτι ἐὰν καταγινώσκῃ ἡμῶν ἡ καρδία, “because if our heart condemns us” — καταγινώσκω, to know against, to accuse: a close relative of γινώσκω from the know family. ὅτι μείζων ἐστὶν ὁ θεὸς τῆς καρδίας ἡμῶν, “God is greater than our heart” — our conscience is not the final judge. καὶ γινώσκει πάντα, “and knows all things” — γινώσκω, from the know family: He knows our sins, but He also knows our love for Him, His Son's sacrifice and His own work in us (John 21:17).","words":[{"strongs":"G2607","hebrew":"καταγινώσκω","translit":"kataginṓskō","english":"condemns"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knows"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Beloved, if our hearts don’t condemn us, we have boldness toward God;","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, “Beloved” — from the love family. ἐὰν ἡ καρδία ἡμῶν μὴ καταγινώσκῃ ἡμῶν, “if our hearts don’t condemn us” — not because we are sinless, but because we are walking in the light and confessing. παρρησίαν ἔχομεν πρὸς τὸν θεόν, “we have boldness toward God” — παρρησία, the confidence of 2:28, now in prayer.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G2607","hebrew":"καταγινώσκω","translit":"kataginṓskō","english":"condemn"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":22,"text":"and whatever we ask, we receive from him, because we keep his commandments and do the things that are pleasing in his sight.","understanding":"καὶ ὃ ἐὰν αἰτῶμεν λαμβάνομεν ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “and whatever we ask, we receive from him” — αἰτέω, from the pray family: to ask as a child asks a father; λαμβάνω, to receive. ὅτι τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ τηροῦμεν, “because we keep his commandments” — τηρέω, from the hold family. καὶ τὰ ἀρεστὰ ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ ποιοῦμεν, “and do the things that are pleasing in his sight” — ἀρεστός, pleasing. Obedience does not earn answers, but it aligns our asking with His will (5:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G701","hebrew":"ἀρεστός","translit":"arestós","english":"pleasing"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in his sight"}]},{"v":23,"text":"This is his commandment, that we should believe in the name of his Son, Jesus Christ, and love one another, even as he commanded.","understanding":"καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ ἐντολὴ αὐτοῦ, “This is his commandment” — singular: one command with two parts. ἵνα πιστεύσωμεν τῷ ὀνόματι τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “that we should believe in the name of his Son, Jesus Christ” — the first mention of faith in the letter, the test of truth. καὶ ἀγαπῶμεν ἀλλήλους, “and love one another” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family, the test of love. καθὼς ἔδωκεν ἐντολὴν ἡμῖν, “even as he commanded” — ἐντέλλομαι. Faith and love are one command.","words":[{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1781","hebrew":"ἐντέλλομαι","translit":"entéllomai","english":"commanded"}]},{"v":24,"text":"He who keeps his commandments remains in him, and he in him. By this we know that he remains in us, by the Spirit which he gave us.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ τηρῶν τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ ἐν αὐτῷ μένει καὶ αὐτὸς ἐν αὐτῷ, “He who keeps his commandments remains in him, and he in him” — τηρέω, from the hold family; μένω both ways: mutual indwelling, as Jesus said (John 15:4). καὶ ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκομεν ὅτι μένει ἐν ἡμῖν, ἐκ τοῦ πνεύματος οὗ ἡμῖν ἔδωκεν, “By this we know that he remains in us, by the Spirit which he gave us” — γινώσκω, from the know family; πνεῦμα, the Holy Spirit, the first mention in the letter: the inner witness of the anointing (2:20, 27) and of Romans 8:16.","words":[{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Called Children","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture an orphan standing in a courtroom, ragged and frightened. The judge reads the adoption order, and a man steps forward, kneels, and says, ‘This is my child.’ He gives the child his name, his home and his inheritance. Years later the child, grown, looks into a mirror and sees, more and more, the face of the father who claimed him.","meaning":"“See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God!” (v. 1). “Beloved, now we are children of God, and it is not yet revealed what we will be. But we know that, when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is” (v. 2). You are already God's child; one day you will fully bear His likeness."},{"title":"Two Families","verseRef":"v. 7-10","image":"Picture two families at a reunion. In one, everyone shares the same kind eyes and warm laugh; in the other, the same cold stare and sharp tongue. You can tell which family a stranger belongs to just by watching how they behave.","meaning":"“In this the children of God are revealed, and the children of the devil. Whoever doesn’t do righteousness is not of God, neither is he who doesn’t love his brother” (v. 10). The family likeness shows. Christ came to destroy the works of the devil (v. 8), and His seed in you produces His likeness: righteousness and love."},{"title":"The Shepherd's Life Laid Down","verseRef":"v. 16","image":"Picture a shepherd on a hillside at night, wolves circling the flock. He steps between the sheep and the wolves, armed only with a staff, knowing he may not walk away. In the morning the flock is safe, and the shepherd lies still among them.","meaning":"“By this we know love, because he laid down his life for us. And we ought to lay down our lives for the brothers” (v. 16). The cross defines love. Most of us will not be asked to die for another believer, but we are asked every day to lay down our time, comfort, money and rights for them."},{"title":"The Closed Door","verseRef":"v. 17-18","image":"Picture a comfortable house on a cold night. Through the window you can see a warm fire and a full table. Outside, a fellow believer knocks, shivering and hungry. Inside, someone glances at the door, recognises the face, and quietly turns the key in the lock.","meaning":"“But whoever has the world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and closes his heart of compassion against him, how does God’s love remain in him?” (v. 17). “My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth” (v. 18). Love is proved at the door, not in the prayer meeting."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Children of God (v. 1-3)","body":"See the Father's great love: we are called and truly are God's children; the world does not know us because it did not know Him; what we will be is not yet revealed, but when He appears we will be like Him, for we will see Him as He is; this hope purifies."},{"label":"Sin and the Son's Mission (v. 4-10)","body":"Sin is lawlessness; Christ appeared to take away sins and in Him is no sin; whoever remains in Him does not go on sinning; whoever practises sin is of the devil, whose works the Son came to destroy; whoever is born of God cannot keep on sinning, because God's seed remains in him; righteousness and love reveal the children of God."},{"label":"Love Versus Hatred (v. 11-18)","body":"The message from the beginning is to love one another, unlike Cain who murdered his brother; do not be surprised if the world hates you; love shows we have passed from death to life, while hatred is murder; Christ laid down His life for us, and we ought to lay down our lives for each other; closing our hearts to a brother in need shows God's love is absent; love must be in deed and truth."},{"label":"Assurance Before God (v. 19-24)","body":"Loving in deed reassures our hearts before God, who is greater than our hearts and knows all things; if our hearts do not condemn us, we have confidence in prayer and receive what we ask, because we obey; His command is to believe in His Son and love one another; whoever obeys remains in Him, and we know He remains in us by the Spirit He has given."}],"sections":[{"heading":"What Love the Father Has Given","paragraphs":["John breaks out in wonder: “See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God!” (v. 1). The word ποταπός suggests a love from another country — utterly unlike anything in this world. God does not merely tolerate us or employ us; He makes us His children. And it is not just a title: “now we are children of God” (v. 2). The world does not recognise this, just as it did not recognise Jesus.","But the best is still to come: “it is not yet revealed what we will be. But we know that, when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is” (v. 2). Paul says the same: “For whom he foreknew, he also predestined to be conformed to the image of his Son” (Romans 8:29), and “When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory” (Colossians 3:4). Seeing Christ will complete the transformation that has already begun.","That hope changes how we live now: “Everyone who has this hope set on him purifies himself, even as he is pure” (v. 3). Jesus said, “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God” (Matthew 5:8). If you expect to see Him, you prepare to look like Him."]},{"heading":"Can a Christian Sin?","paragraphs":["Verses 4–10 contain some of the most puzzling statements in the letter. “Whoever remains in him doesn’t sin” (v. 6). “Whoever is born of God doesn’t commit sin, because his seed remains in him; and he can’t sin, because he is born of God” (v. 9). Yet John has already said, “If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves” (1:8), and “If anyone sins, we have a Counselor with the Father” (2:1). How do these fit together?","The strongest answer lies in the Greek present tense, which describes ongoing, habitual action. John is not saying that a believer never commits an act of sin, but that a child of God cannot settle into sin as a way of life — cannot practise it without grief, confession and change. The reason is God's ‘seed’: His life planted in the believer, which fights against sin from within. Paul asks, “Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound?” (Romans 6:1) and answers, “May it never be! We who died to sin, how could we live in it any longer?” (Romans 6:2). The false teachers apparently said sin did not matter; John says it matters supremely, because it is lawlessness (v. 4), it is the devil's work (v. 8), and Christ came to take it away (v. 5).","So the test is direction, not perfection. “To this end the Son of God was revealed: that he might destroy the works of the devil” (v. 8). A person who is comfortable in sin, who defends it and makes no fight against it, shows the family likeness of the devil, who ‘has been sinning from the beginning’. A person who grieves over sin, confesses it and keeps turning towards righteousness and love shows the likeness of the Father (v. 10)."]},{"heading":"Love in Deed and Truth","paragraphs":["The message from the beginning is “that we should love one another” (v. 11). John's negative example is Cain, who killed his brother “Because his deeds were evil, and his brother’s righteous” (v. 12). Hatred of goodness is the devil's own spirit. So believers should not be surprised when the world hates them (v. 13). Hebrews says Abel, “being dead, still speaks” (Hebrews 11:4).","Love is the evidence of new life: “We know that we have passed out of death into life, because we love the brothers” (v. 14). Jesus used the same words: “he who hears my word, and believes him who sent me, has eternal life, and doesn’t come into judgment, but has passed out of death into life” (John 5:24). Hatred, by contrast, is murder in seed form (v. 15), as Jesus taught in the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5:21–22).","Then John defines love by the cross: “By this we know love, because he laid down his life for us. And we ought to lay down our lives for the brothers” (v. 16). And he brings it down to earth: “But whoever has the world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and closes his heart of compassion against him, how does God’s love remain in him?” (v. 17). Notice the three Greek words for life in verses 14–17: ζωή, the eternal life we have received; ψυχή, the self Christ laid down; βίος, the possessions we are asked to share. If we will not share our βίος, our claim to be willing to give our ψυχή is empty. “My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth” (v. 18). James said the same: words of blessing without bread help no one (James 2:15–16)."]},{"heading":"When Our Hearts Condemn Us","paragraphs":["Tender consciences can be troubled by such high standards. John speaks to them: “And by this we know that we are of the truth, and persuade our hearts before him, because if our heart condemns us, God is greater than our heart, and knows all things” (vv. 19–20). Real acts of love are evidence that we belong to the truth. And when our conscience still accuses us, we appeal to a higher court: God knows everything — our sin, yes, but also our faith in His Son, our love for His people and His own work in us. Peter, grieved, said to Jesus, ‘Lord, you know everything. You know that I have affection for you’ (John 21:17).","When our hearts are at rest, “we have boldness toward God; and whatever we ask, we receive from him, because we keep his commandments” (vv. 21–22). Jesus promised, “All things, whatever you ask in prayer, believing, you will receive” (Matthew 21:22). Obedience does not buy answers; it brings our desires into line with God's.","John then sums up the whole letter in one command with two parts: “that we should believe in the name of his Son, Jesus Christ, and love one another” (v. 23). Faith and love belong together. And the final assurance is the Spirit: “By this we know that he remains in us, by the Spirit which he gave us” (v. 24). Paul says, “The Spirit himself testifies with our spirit that we are children of God” (Romans 8:16)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Children, the Destroyer of the Devil's Works, and the Good Shepherd","paragraphs":["Verse 8 is one of the great mission statements of Christ: He was revealed “that he might destroy the works of the devil.” Hebrews describes how: “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil” (Hebrews 2:14). Jesus described the devil in the same terms John uses: “He was a murderer from the beginning, and doesn’t stand in the truth, because there is no truth in him” (John 8:44).","Verse 16 echoes Jesus' own words: “Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends” (John 15:13), and “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep” (John 10:11). The same verb, τίθημι, ‘lay down’, is used in each place. John watched the Good Shepherd do it.","The transformation of verse 2 is described by Paul: “But we all, with unveiled face seeing the glory of the Lord as in a mirror, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, even as from the Lord, the Spirit” (2 Corinthians 3:18). And the assurance of verse 20 — God greater than our hearts — finds its fullest expression in Romans: “There is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus” (Romans 8:1)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"How great / what manner","hebrew":"ποταπός","translit":"potapós","strongs":"G4217 (children τέκνον, téknon, G5043)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “interrogatively, whatever, i.e. of what possible sort”. The word grew from an older form that asked ‘from what land?’ — used of something foreign and astonishing. John uses it of the Father's love: a love from another world, unlike anything human. Its gift is that we are called τέκνα θεοῦ, children of God, from the children family — family by birth, not just by name. The world cannot understand this love, because it never understood the Son who revealed it."},{"english":"Lawlessness","hebrew":"ἀνομία","translit":"anomía","strongs":"G458 (sin ἁμαρτία, hamartía, G266)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “illegality, i.e. violation of law”. ἀνομία is from the sin family, meaning ‘without law’: living as if God's law did not exist. John defines sin itself as lawlessness, refusing any view of sin as merely a weakness or an illness. Jesus will say to false prophets, ‘Depart from me, you who work ἀνομία’ (Matthew 7:23). Every sin, however small it seems, is a declaration of independence from God."},{"english":"Seed","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","strongs":"G4690 (born γεννάω, gennáō, G1080)","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “something sown, i.e. seed (including the male \"sperm\"); by implication, offspring; specially, a remnant”. σπέρμα is God's life implanted in the believer — the divine nature (2 Peter 1:4), the incorruptible seed of the word (1 Peter 1:23), the Spirit Himself. Because this seed remains, the believer cannot comfortably settle into sin. Like produces like: those born (γεννάω, from the birth family) of God bear His likeness."},{"english":"World’s goods / life","hebrew":"βίος","translit":"bíos","strongs":"G979 (life ζωή, zōḗ, G2222; life ψυχή, psychḗ, G5590)","verses":"v. 14, 16, 17; 2:16","insight":"Strong's: “life, i.e. (literally) the present state of existence; by implication, the means of livelihood.” Verses 14–17 use all three words from the life family. ζωή (v. 14) is the divine life believers receive; ψυχή (v. 16) is the natural self, which Christ laid down; βίος (v. 17) is the means of life — money, food, possessions. The argument is from greater to lesser: if Christ gave His ψυχή, and we have received ζωή, how can we refuse to share our βίος?"},{"english":"Condemn","hebrew":"καταγινώσκω","translit":"kataginṓskō","strongs":"G2607 (know γινώσκω, ginṓskō, G1097)","verses":"v. 20, 21","insight":"Strong's: “to note against, i.e. find fault with.” καταγινώσκω is γινώσκω (to know) with κατά (against): a knowledge that accuses. Our heart ‘knows against’ us. But God, who also γινώσκει πάντα, ‘knows all things’, is greater. Conscience is a real witness, but not the final judge. For a believer walking in the light, God's knowledge of the cross and of His own work in us overrules our self-condemnation."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit wants you to see the Father's love, take sin seriously, and love your brothers and sisters in costly, practical ways.","items":[{"title":"Look at the Father's Love","body":"“See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God!” (v. 1). Do you see yourself as God's child, or only as His servant or His project? The Spirit wants you to stop and look at His love. Spend time today simply receiving it, and let it change how you see yourself."},{"title":"Is There a Sin You Are Comfortable With?","body":"John says that a child of God cannot settle into sin as a way of life. Is there a sin you have stopped fighting, excused or made peace with? The Spirit is not condemning you; He is reminding you that the Son of God came “that he might destroy the works of the devil” (v. 8). Ask Him to destroy that work in you."},{"title":"Whose Need Have You Seen?","body":"“But whoever has the world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and closes his heart of compassion against him, how does God’s love remain in him?” (v. 17). The Spirit brings someone to mind — a believer in material, emotional or practical need. What part of your βίος — your money, time, home or skills — is He asking you to share this week?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Identity Check","body":"Do you live as a child of God, secure in the Father's love, or as an orphan who must earn approval (vv. 1–2)?"},{"title":"The Purity Check","body":"Does your hope of seeing Christ make you purify yourself (v. 3)? Is there a pattern of sin you have stopped resisting (vv. 6–9)?"},{"title":"The Deed Check","body":"When did your love last cost you something in money, time or comfort (vv. 16–18)? Is there a brother or sister in need to whom you have closed your heart?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Lay Down Something This Week","body":"Choose one concrete way to ‘lay down your life’ for a fellow believer this week (v. 16): give money to meet a need, spend an evening helping someone move or repair something, visit someone lonely, or give up something you wanted so another can have it. Do it quietly, in deed and truth."},{"title":"Answer a Condemning Heart","body":"When your conscience accuses you after you have confessed, read verses 19–20 aloud. Write down evidence of God's work in you — faith in His Son, love for His people, desire to obey. Then pray, ‘Lord, You are greater than my heart, and You know all things.’ Rest in His verdict."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, see how great a love You have given me, that I should be called Your child — and that is what I am. Thank You that one day, when Christ appears, I will be like Him, for I will see Him as He is. Let that hope purify me now. I confess that sin is lawlessness, and I do not want to make peace with any of it. Thank You that Jesus was revealed to take away my sins and to destroy the works of the devil. Let Your seed in me grow, so that I bear Your family likeness in righteousness and love. Thank You that I have passed from death into life. Teach me love as Jesus showed it, laying down His life for me, and make me willing to lay down my life — my time, my comfort, my goods — for my brothers and sisters. Never let me close my heart to someone in need. Let my love be in deed and truth, not only in words. When my heart condemns me, remind me that You are greater than my heart and know all things. Give me confidence in prayer as I keep Your commands, believe in the name of Your Son and love one another. Thank You for the Spirit You have given me, by whom I know You remain in me. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Cain and Abel","ref":"Genesis 4:1-16; Hebrews 11:4","relatedVerses":"v. 11-15","summary":"Cain and Abel both brought offerings to God. God accepted Abel's but not Cain's. Instead of turning to God, Cain grew angry, and God warned him that sin was crouching at the door, desiring to have him. Cain did not master it. He lured his brother into the field and killed him. When God asked, ‘Where is Abel your brother?’ Cain answered, ‘Am I my brother's keeper?’ John says Cain hated Abel because Abel's deeds were righteous and his own were evil. Hatred, envy and murder are the family likeness of the evil one; love is the likeness of God."},{"title":"The Good Samaritan","ref":"Luke 10:25-37","relatedVerses":"v. 16-18","summary":"A man was beaten by robbers and left half dead on the road. A priest saw him and passed by on the other side; so did a Levite. Then a Samaritan — a despised outsider — saw him, was moved with compassion, bandaged his wounds, put him on his own animal, took him to an inn and paid for his care. Jesus asked who proved to be a neighbour, and said, ‘Go and do likewise.’ The priest and Levite closed their hearts; the Samaritan opened his heart and his purse. That is the difference between love in word and love in deed."},{"title":"Peter: ‘Lord, You Know Everything’","ref":"John 21:15-17","relatedVerses":"v. 19-20","summary":"After Peter's denial, the risen Jesus asked him three times, ‘Do you love me?’ The third time, Peter was grieved, perhaps feeling the weight of his three denials. His answer appealed to Jesus' greater knowledge: “Lord, you know everything. You know that I have affection for you” (John 21:17). His own heart could condemn him, but Jesus knew the truth about his love. John was there. He writes that when our hearts condemn us, God is greater than our hearts and knows all things."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 John","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Testing the Spirits, and God Who Is Love","themeTitle":"“God Is Love”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world.","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, “Beloved” — from the love family. μὴ παντὶ πνεύματι πιστεύετε, “don’t believe every spirit” — πνεῦμα, here a spirit speaking through a prophet or teacher. ἀλλὰ δοκιμάζετε τὰ πνεύματα εἰ ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστιν, “but test the spirits, whether they are of God” — δοκιμάζω, from the test family: to assay like metal, to prove genuine or false. ὅτι πολλοὶ ψευδοπροφῆται ἐξεληλύθασιν εἰς τὸν κόσμον, “because many false prophets have gone out into the world” — ψευδοπροφήτης, the false prophets Peter also warned of in his last letter; κόσμος, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"test"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5578","hebrew":"ψευδοπροφήτης","translit":"pseudoprophḗtēs","english":"false prophets"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"gone out"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":2,"text":"By this you know the Spirit of God: every spirit who confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God,","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκετε τὸ πνεῦμα τοῦ θεοῦ, “By this you know the Spirit of God” — γινώσκω, from the know family. πᾶν πνεῦμα ὃ ὁμολογεῖ Ἰησοῦν Χριστὸν ἐν σαρκὶ ἐληλυθότα ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστιν, “every spirit who confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God” — ὁμολογέω, to confess, to say the same as God; σάρξ, real human flesh; ἐληλυθότα, perfect tense: came and remains in that incarnate state. The test is Christ's person.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confesses"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"}]},{"v":3,"text":"and every spirit who doesn’t confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is not of God, and this is the spirit of the Antichrist, of whom you have heard that it comes. Now it is in the world already.","understanding":"καὶ πᾶν πνεῦμα ὃ μὴ ὁμολογεῖ τὸν Ἰησοῦν, “and every spirit who doesn’t confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is not of God” — the WEB follows manuscripts that repeat the full confession. καὶ τοῦτό ἐστιν τὸ τοῦ ἀντιχρίστου, “and this is the spirit of the Antichrist” — ἀντίχριστος, as in 2:18, 22. “of whom you have heard that it comes. Now it is in the world already.” — κόσμος, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confess"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G500","hebrew":"ἀντίχριστος","translit":"antíchristos","english":"Antichrist"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":4,"text":"You are of God, little children, and have overcome them; because greater is he who is in you than he who is in the world.","understanding":"ὑμεῖς ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστε, τεκνία, “You are of God, little children” — τεκνίον, from the children family. καὶ νενικήκατε αὐτούς, “and have overcome them” — νικάω, perfect tense: the victory is won and stands. ὅτι μείζων ἐστὶν ὁ ἐν ὑμῖν ἢ ὁ ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ, “because greater is he who is in you than he who is in the world” — the Spirit of God in believers is greater than the spirit of antichrist; κόσμος, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"little children"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":5,"text":"They are of the world. Therefore they speak of the world, and the world hears them.","understanding":"αὐτοὶ ἐκ τοῦ κόσμου εἰσίν, “They are of the world” — κόσμος, from the world family, three times. διὰ τοῦτο ἐκ τοῦ κόσμου λαλοῦσιν, “Therefore they speak of the world” — their message comes from the world's way of thinking. καὶ ὁ κόσμος αὐτῶν ἀκούει, “and the world hears them” — popularity is not proof of truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hears"}]},{"v":6,"text":"We are of God. He who knows God listens to us. He who is not of God doesn’t listen to us. By this we know the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error.","understanding":"ἡμεῖς ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐσμεν, “We are of God” — ‘we’ here are the apostles. ὁ γινώσκων τὸν θεὸν ἀκούει ἡμῶν, “He who knows God listens to us” — γινώσκω, from the know family. The apostolic teaching is the standard. ἐκ τούτου γινώσκομεν τὸ πνεῦμα τῆς ἀληθείας καὶ τὸ πνεῦμα τῆς πλάνης, “By this we know the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error” — πλάνη, deception, the ‘error’ of 2 Peter 3:17.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"listens"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G4106","hebrew":"πλάνη","translit":"plánē","english":"error"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God; and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God.","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, ἀγαπῶμεν ἀλλήλους, “Beloved, let us love one another” — ἀγαπητός and ἀγαπάω, both from the love family: the beloved must love. ὅτι ἡ ἀγάπη ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐστιν, “for love is of God” — ἀγάπη, from the love family: love has its source in God. καὶ πᾶς ὁ ἀγαπῶν ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ γεγέννηται καὶ γινώσκει τὸν θεόν, “and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God” — γεννάω, from the birth family; γινώσκω, from the know family. Love is the family likeness of the newborn.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knows"}]},{"v":8,"text":"He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love.","understanding":"ὁ μὴ ἀγαπῶν οὐκ ἔγνω τὸν θεόν, “He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; γινώσκω, from the know family. ὅτι ὁ θεὸς ἀγάπη ἐστίν, “for God is love” — ἀγάπη, from the love family. Not ‘love is God’, as if every feeling of love were divine, but ‘God is love’: His whole being is self-giving love, defined by the cross (v. 10). The same Greek construction as ‘God is light’ (1:5) and ‘God is Spirit’ (John 4:24).","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"}]},{"v":9,"text":"By this God’s love was revealed in us, that God has sent his one and only Son into the world that we might live through him.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ ἐφανερώθη ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ θεοῦ ἐν ἡμῖν, “By this God’s love was revealed in us” — φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible; ἀγάπη, from the love family. ὅτι τὸν υἱὸν αὐτοῦ τὸν μονογενῆ ἀπέσταλκεν ὁ θεὸς εἰς τὸν κόσμον, “that God has sent his one and only Son into the world” — μονογενής, unique, one of a kind; ἀποστέλλω, to send on a mission; κόσμος, from the world family. ἵνα ζήσωμεν δι᾽ αὐτοῦ, “that we might live through him” — ζάω, from the life family. The letter's own version of John 3:16.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G3439","hebrew":"μονογενής","translit":"monogenḗs","english":"one and only"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"}]},{"v":10,"text":"In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ ἐστὶν ἡ ἀγάπη, “In this is love” — ἀγάπη, from the love family: here is its definition. οὐχ ὅτι ἡμεῖς ἠγαπήκαμεν τὸν θεόν, ἀλλ᾽ ὅτι αὐτὸς ἠγάπησεν ἡμᾶς, “not that we loved God, but that he loved us” — ἀγαπάω, also from the love family: love begins with God, not us. καὶ ἀπέστειλεν τὸν υἱὸν αὐτοῦ ἱλασμὸν περὶ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν, “and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins” — ἱλασμός, from the offering family, as in 2:2; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. God's love is not sentimental; it is holy love that pays for sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2434","hebrew":"ἱλασμός","translit":"hilasmós","english":"atoning sacrifice"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Beloved, if God loved us in this way, we also ought to love one another.","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, εἰ οὕτως ὁ θεὸς ἠγάπησεν ἡμᾶς, “Beloved, if God loved us in this way” — ἀγαπητός and ἀγαπάω, from the love family; οὕτως, ‘so’, ‘in this way’ — the way of the cross. καὶ ἡμεῖς ὀφείλομεν ἀλλήλους ἀγαπᾶν, “we also ought to love one another” — ὀφείλω, to owe: we owe others the love God has given us.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"},{"strongs":"G3784","hebrew":"ὀφείλω","translit":"opheílō","english":"ought"}]},{"v":12,"text":"No one has seen God at any time. If we love one another, God remains in us, and his love has been perfected in us.","understanding":"θεὸν οὐδεὶς πώποτε τεθέαται, “No one has seen God at any time” — θεάομαι, from the see family: to gaze upon, as in 1:1; the words of John 1:18. ἐὰν ἀγαπῶμεν ἀλλήλους, ὁ θεὸς ἐν ἡμῖν μένει, “If we love one another, God remains in us” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; μένω. The invisible God becomes visible in His people's love. καὶ ἡ ἀγάπη αὐτοῦ τετελειωμένη ἐν ἡμῖν ἐστιν, “and his love has been perfected in us” — τελειόω, from the perfect family: brought to its goal.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2300","hebrew":"θεάομαι","translit":"theáomai","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"perfected"}]},{"v":13,"text":"By this we know that we remain in him and he in us, because he has given us of his Spirit.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκομεν ὅτι ἐν αὐτῷ μένομεν καὶ αὐτὸς ἐν ἡμῖν, “By this we know that we remain in him and he in us” — γινώσκω, from the know family; μένω. ὅτι ἐκ τοῦ πνεύματος αὐτοῦ δέδωκεν ἡμῖν, “because he has given us of his Spirit” — the Spirit is the inner evidence of union with God, as in 3:24.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"}]},{"v":14,"text":"We have seen and testify that the Father has sent the Son as the Savior of the world.","understanding":"καὶ ἡμεῖς τεθεάμεθα καὶ μαρτυροῦμεν, “We have seen and testify” — θεάομαι again, from the see family: no one has seen God (v. 12), but the apostles gazed upon the Son. μαρτυρέω, to bear witness. ὅτι ὁ πατὴρ ἀπέσταλκεν τὸν υἱὸν σωτῆρα τοῦ κόσμου, “that the Father has sent the Son as the Savior of the world” — σωτήρ; κόσμος, from the world family: the Samaritans' confession (John 4:42). μαρτυρέω is from the witness family — to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2300","hebrew":"θεάομαι","translit":"theáomai","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Whoever confesses that Jesus is the Son of God, God remains in him, and he in God.","understanding":"ὃς ἐὰν ὁμολογήσῃ ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, “Whoever confesses that Jesus is the Son of God” — ὁμολογέω, to confess: the test of truth (v. 2). ὁ θεὸς ἐν αὐτῷ μένει καὶ αὐτὸς ἐν τῷ θεῷ, “God remains in him, and he in God” — μένω both ways.","words":[{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confesses"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"}]},{"v":16,"text":"We know and have believed the love which God has for us. God is love, and he who remains in love remains in God, and God remains in him.","understanding":"καὶ ἡμεῖς ἐγνώκαμεν καὶ πεπιστεύκαμεν τὴν ἀγάπην ἣν ἔχει ὁ θεὸς ἐν ἡμῖν, “We know and have believed the love which God has for us” — γινώσκω, from the know family, and πιστεύω: knowing and believing together; ἀγάπη, from the love family. ὁ θεὸς ἀγάπη ἐστίν, “God is love” — the second time (v. 8). καὶ ὁ μένων ἐν τῇ ἀγάπῃ ἐν τῷ θεῷ μένει καὶ ὁ θεὸς ἐν αὐτῷ μένει, “and he who remains in love remains in God, and God remains in him” — μένω three times. To live in love is to live in God's home.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"}]},{"v":17,"text":"In this love has been made perfect among us, that we may have boldness in the day of judgment, because as he is, even so are we in this world.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ τετελείωται ἡ ἀγάπη μεθ᾽ ἡμῶν, “In this love has been made perfect among us” — τελειόω, from the perfect family; ἀγάπη, from the love family. ἵνα παρρησίαν ἔχωμεν ἐν τῇ ἡμέρᾳ τῆς κρίσεως, “that we may have boldness in the day of judgment” — παρρησία, the confidence of 2:28; ἡμέρα, from the time family. ὅτι καθὼς ἐκεῖνός ἐστιν καὶ ἡμεῖς ἐσμεν ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ τούτῳ, “because as he is, even so are we in this world” — κόσμος, from the world family: as the Son is loved by the Father, so are we; as He loves, so we love. κρίσις is from the judge family — judgement as an act or process — the trial, the hour of judging.","words":[{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"made perfect"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"}]},{"v":18,"text":"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear, because fear has punishment. He who fears is not made perfect in love.","understanding":"φόβος οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τῇ ἀγάπῃ, “There is no fear in love” — φόβος, terror, the dread of punishment, not the reverent fear of God (1 Peter 1:17); ἀγάπη, from the love family. ἀλλ᾽ ἡ τελεία ἀγάπη ἔξω βάλλει τὸν φόβον, “but perfect love casts out fear” — τέλειος, from the perfect family: mature, complete; βάλλω ἔξω, to throw out, as one throws out an intruder. ὅτι ὁ φόβος κόλασιν ἔχει, “because fear has punishment” — κόλασις, punishment, the word Jesus used of final judgement (Matthew 25:46). ὁ δὲ φοβούμενος οὐ τετελείωται ἐν τῇ ἀγάπῃ, “He who fears is not made perfect in love” — τελειόω, also from the perfect family: the adjective and the verb side by side.","words":[{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5046","hebrew":"τέλειος","translit":"téleios","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"casts out"},{"strongs":"G2851","hebrew":"κόλασις","translit":"kólasis","english":"punishment"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fears"},{"strongs":"G5048","hebrew":"τελειόω","translit":"teleióō","english":"made perfect"}]},{"v":19,"text":"We love him, because he first loved us.","understanding":"ἡμεῖς ἀγαπῶμεν, ὅτι αὐτὸς πρῶτος ἠγάπησεν ἡμᾶς, “We love him, because he first loved us” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family, twice. ‘Him’ is not in the earliest manuscripts, which read simply ‘We love, because he first loved us’: all our love — for God and for others — is a response to His. πρῶτος, first: God always takes the initiative.","words":[{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"}]},{"v":20,"text":"If a man says, “I love God,” and hates his brother, he is a liar; for he who doesn’t love his brother whom he has seen, how can he love God whom he has not seen?","understanding":"ἐάν τις εἴπῃ ὅτι Ἀγαπῶ τὸν θεόν, καὶ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ μισῇ, ψεύστης ἐστίν, “If a man says, ‘I love God,’ and hates his brother, he is a liar” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; μισέω, to hate. ὁ γὰρ μὴ ἀγαπῶν τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ ὃν ἑώρακεν, τὸν θεὸν ὃν οὐχ ἑώρακεν οὐ δύναται ἀγαπᾶν, “for he who doesn’t love his brother whom he has seen, how can he love God whom he has not seen?” — ὁράω, from the see family, twice: the visible brother is the test of love for the invisible God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2036","hebrew":"ἔπω","translit":"épō","english":"says"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hates"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liar"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"}]},{"v":21,"text":"This commandment we have from him, that he who loves God should also love his brother.","understanding":"καὶ ταύτην τὴν ἐντολὴν ἔχομεν ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “This commandment we have from him” — ἐντολή, the commandment. ἵνα ὁ ἀγαπῶν τὸν θεὸν ἀγαπᾷ καὶ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ, “that he who loves God should also love his brother” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family: the two great commandments cannot be separated (Matthew 22:37–39).","words":[{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Assayer's Test","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a goldsmith receiving coins from many travellers. Some are real gold; some are lead painted gold. He does not judge by shine or by the confidence of the man handing them over. He scratches each coin on his touchstone and sees what lies beneath.","meaning":"“Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world” (v. 1). The touchstone is Christ: “every spirit who confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God” (v. 2). Do not judge teachers by their eloquence, popularity or confidence. Scratch them on the truth about Jesus."},{"title":"The Love That Came Down","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a father whose only son volunteers to go into a burning building to rescue strangers who have wronged them both. The father watches him go, knowing what it will cost. The strangers are carried out alive. The son does not come back.","meaning":"“By this God’s love was revealed in us, that God has sent his one and only Son into the world that we might live through him” (v. 9). “In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins” (v. 10). Love is not defined by our feelings for God, but by His gift of His Son for us."},{"title":"The Invisible God Made Visible","verseRef":"v. 12","image":"Picture the wind. You cannot see it, but you see the trees bend, the grass ripple, the sails fill. Now picture a church where people forgive one another, care for the sick, welcome strangers and give to the poor — and an unbeliever watching says, ‘There is Someone here.’","meaning":"“No one has seen God at any time. If we love one another, God remains in us, and his love has been perfected in us” (v. 12). The world cannot see God directly, but it can see His love in His people. Your love for others is how the invisible God becomes visible in your street."},{"title":"Fear Thrown Out","verseRef":"v. 17-18","image":"Picture a child who has broken something precious, hiding in a cupboard, terrified of punishment. Then picture the same child running into the arms of a parent who has already forgiven everything, burying her face in their chest, no longer afraid.","meaning":"“There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear, because fear has punishment” (v. 18). God's perfected love does not leave His children cowering before judgement; it gives them “boldness in the day of judgment” (v. 17). Not because they are good, but because He is love, and He has dealt with their sin."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Test the Spirits (v. 1-6)","body":"Do not believe every spirit but test them, for many false prophets have gone out; every spirit that confesses Jesus Christ come in the flesh is from God, and every spirit that does not is the spirit of antichrist; believers have overcome them, because the One in them is greater than the one in the world; the false teachers are from the world and the world listens to them; whoever knows God listens to the apostles."},{"label":"God Is Love (v. 7-12)","body":"Let us love one another, for love comes from God; whoever loves is born of God and knows Him, whoever does not love does not know God, for God is love; His love was revealed in sending His only Son that we might live, as the atoning sacrifice for our sins; since God so loved us, we ought to love one another; no one has seen God, but if we love one another He remains in us and His love is perfected in us."},{"label":"Remaining in Love (v. 13-16)","body":"We know we remain in Him because He has given us His Spirit; the apostles have seen and testify that the Father sent the Son as Saviour of the world; whoever confesses Jesus as Son of God remains in God; God is love, and whoever remains in love remains in God."},{"label":"Love Casts Out Fear (v. 17-21)","body":"Love is perfected among us so that we may have confidence on the day of judgement; there is no fear in love, for perfect love casts out fear; we love because He first loved us; whoever claims to love God but hates his brother is a liar, for whoever does not love the brother he has seen cannot love God whom he has not seen; whoever loves God must also love his brother."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Test the Spirits","paragraphs":["The early church valued prophecy — believers speaking under the Spirit's inspiration. But not every voice claiming the Spirit was genuine. “Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world” (v. 1). Paul said the same: “Test all things, and hold firmly that which is good” (1 Thessalonians 5:21). Jesus warned, “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep’s clothing” (Matthew 7:15).","John gives the test: “every spirit who confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God” (v. 2). The false teachers denied the incarnation — that the eternal Son truly became a man. Any teaching that denies either the full deity or the full humanity of Jesus is not from God. Paul gives a parallel test: “No one can say, ‘Jesus is Lord,’ but by the Holy Spirit” (1 Corinthians 12:3).","John also gives believers confidence: “You are of God, little children, and have overcome them; because greater is he who is in you than he who is in the world” (v. 4). The false teachers had a large audience — “the world hears them” (v. 5) — but popularity is not truth. The test is whether a teaching agrees with the apostles: “He who knows God listens to us” (v. 6). For us, that means agreeing with the apostolic Scriptures."]},{"heading":"God Is Love — and What That Means","paragraphs":["Then John reaches the summit of the letter: “Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God; and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God. He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love” (vv. 7–8). This is one of the most quoted sentences in the Bible, and one of the most misused. John does not say ‘love is God’, as if every human feeling of love were divine. He says ‘God is love’ — love is the very nature of God, and all true love comes from Him.","And John defines that love immediately, so that no one can turn it into mere sentiment: “By this God’s love was revealed in us, that God has sent his one and only Son into the world that we might live through him” (v. 9). “In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins” (v. 10). Love is not first our love for God, but God's love for us — a love that gave its best, at its own cost, for those who did not deserve it. It is holy love: it deals with sin by providing a sacrifice. Jesus said, “For God so loved the world, that he gave his one and only Son” (John 3:16). Paul: “But God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us” (Romans 5:8).","The application is direct: “Beloved, if God loved us in this way, we also ought to love one another” (v. 11). And the result is remarkable: “No one has seen God at any time. If we love one another, God remains in us, and his love has been perfected in us” (v. 12). God is invisible, but His love becomes visible when His people love one another. John's Gospel says, “No one has seen God at any time. The one and only Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has declared him” (John 1:18). The Son made God visible; now the church is to do the same."]},{"heading":"Perfect Love Casts Out Fear","paragraphs":["The assurance of the believer rests on three witnesses in this chapter: the Spirit God has given (v. 13), the confession that Jesus is the Son of God (v. 15), and the life of love (v. 16). “We know and have believed the love which God has for us. God is love, and he who remains in love remains in God, and God remains in him” (v. 16).","That assurance reaches all the way to judgement day: “In this love has been made perfect among us, that we may have boldness in the day of judgment” (v. 17). And it drives out terror: “There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear, because fear has punishment. He who fears is not made perfect in love” (v. 18). This is not the reverent awe that believers rightly have towards God, but the dread of a criminal awaiting sentence. Paul says, “For you didn’t receive the spirit of bondage again to fear, but you received the Spirit of adoption, by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’” (Romans 8:15). God's children are not cowering slaves.","Finally John brings love back to earth one more time: “We love him, because he first loved us” (v. 19). “If a man says, ‘I love God,’ and hates his brother, he is a liar; for he who doesn’t love his brother whom he has seen, how can he love God whom he has not seen?” (v. 20). Love for God is tested by love for the people He made and saved. Jesus joined the two great commandments: “You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart” and “You shall love your neighbor as yourself” (Matthew 22:37–39). They cannot be separated."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Love of God in Christ","paragraphs":["Verse 9 is John's letter-form of his most famous Gospel verse: “For God so loved the world, that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life” (John 3:16), followed by “For God didn’t send his Son into the world to judge the world, but that the world should be saved through him” (John 3:17). The ‘Savior of the world’ of verse 14 is the confession of the Samaritan villagers: “this is indeed the Christ, the Savior of the world” (John 4:42).","Paul describes how this love reaches us: “God’s love has been poured out into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who was given to us” (Romans 5:5), and prays that believers may “know Christ’s love which surpasses knowledge” (Ephesians 3:19). He makes it personal: “the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself up for me” (Galatians 2:20). And he describes its shape: “Love is patient and is kind; love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud” (1 Corinthians 13:4).","The casting out of fear (v. 18) is echoed by Paul to Timothy: “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (2 Timothy 1:7), and by Hebrews: “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy” (Hebrews 4:16). The prodigal son came home expecting to be a servant; but “while he was still far off, his father saw him, and was moved with compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him” (Luke 15:20). That is perfect love casting out fear."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Test","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","strongs":"G1381 (false prophets ψευδοπροφήτης, pseudoprophḗtēs, G5578)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “to test (literally or figuratively); by implication, to approve.” δοκιμάζω is from the test family — the assayer's word for proving metal. It is the same word Peter used of faith tested by fire (1 Peter 1:7). John tells believers to assay every spirit — every message claiming divine inspiration. The touchstone is the confession of Jesus Christ come in the flesh. Testing is not cynicism; it is love for the truth and for the church."},{"english":"Love","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","strongs":"G26 (love ἀγαπάω, agapáō, G25)","verses":"v. 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 16, 17, 18","insight":"Strong's: “love, i.e. affection or benevolence; specially (plural) a love-feast.” ἀγάπη and ἀγαπάω appear more often in 1 John 4 than in any other chapter of the Bible. In the love family, ἀγάπη is the self-giving love that seeks another's good at its own cost, as distinct from φιλία, warm affection. John defines it not by feeling but by the cross: God sending His Son as an atoning sacrifice (v. 10). ‘God is love’ means that this kind of love is the very nature of God."},{"english":"One and only","hebrew":"μονογενής","translit":"monogenḗs","strongs":"G3439","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “only-born, i.e. sole.” μονογενής, from μόνος (only) and γένος (kind), means unique, one of a kind. It is used of an only child (Luke 7:12) and of Isaac as Abraham's unique son of promise (Hebrews 11:17). John uses it of Jesus five times. It stresses not His origin in time but His unique relationship with the Father: there is no other Son like Him. That is what makes God's love so great: He gave the one and only."},{"english":"Seen / beheld","hebrew":"θεάομαι","translit":"theáomai","strongs":"G2300 (seen ὁράω, horáō, G3708)","verses":"v. 12, 14, 20","insight":"Strong's: “to look closely at, i.e. (by implication) perceive (literally or figuratively); by extension to visit.” θεάομαι, from the see family, is a long, attentive gaze. ‘No one has θεάομαι God’ (v. 12) — but ‘we have θεάομαι’ the Son sent as Saviour (v. 14). In verse 20 John switches to ὁράω, the ordinary word for seeing: the brother you have seen. The invisible God has been seen in the Son, and is seen today in the love of His people."},{"english":"Fear","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","strongs":"G5401 (perfect τέλειος, téleios, G5046; punishment κόλασις, kólasis, G2851)","verses":"v. 18","insight":"Strong's: “alarm or fright.” φόβος can mean reverent awe (1 Peter 1:17) or terror. Here it is the terror of punishment — κόλασις, the word Jesus used of final judgement. John says τελεία ἀγάπη, ‘perfect love’ — from the perfect family, love brought to its goal — throws this fear out. A child who knows the Father's love may still tremble with reverence, but no longer cowers in dread."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit wants to protect you from deception and immerse you in the love of the God who is love.","items":[{"title":"Test What You Hear","body":"You hear many voices claiming to speak for God — preachers, prophets, books, videos, friends. The Spirit asks you not to believe every spirit but to test them (v. 1). The first test: what do they say about Jesus Christ? Do they confess Him as the eternal Son who truly became man? Ask Him to sharpen your discernment."},{"title":"Receive His Love First","body":"“In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us” (v. 10). Many believers try hard to love God and others while running on empty. The Spirit invites you to stop and receive. Before you try to love anyone today, sit with the cross and let His love fill you. “We love him, because he first loved us” (v. 19)."},{"title":"What Are You Afraid Of?","body":"Do you live with a background fear of God's judgement, rejection or punishment? “There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear” (v. 18). The Spirit wants to throw out that fear and replace it with the confidence of a loved child. Name your fear before Him, and hear Him say, ‘I have loved you. I sent My Son for you.’"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Discernment Check","body":"Do you test the teaching you hear by what it says about Jesus and by Scripture (vv. 1–6)? Or do you accept whatever sounds spiritual or popular?"},{"title":"The Source Check","body":"Is your love for others flowing from God's love for you (vv. 10–11, 19), or from duty, guilt or a need to be liked?"},{"title":"The Visible Brother Check","body":"Is there a brother or sister you can see whom you are not loving (v. 20)? How does that affect your claim to love God?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Week in 1 John 4:7–21","body":"Each morning this week read verses 7–21 slowly. Underline every time the words love, God and remain appear. Then choose one sentence to carry through the day, and pray it over the people you meet. In the evening, thank God specifically for one way His love reached you that day."},{"title":"Make God Visible","body":"This week, plan one act of love for a fellow believer that no one will see except them and God (v. 12). Then plan one act of love for someone outside the church. Ask God to let His invisible love become visible through you."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, You are love. Thank You that You loved me first, not because I loved You, but because You chose to send Your one and only Son into the world as the atoning sacrifice for my sins, so that I might live through Him. Fill me with that love until it overflows. Give me discernment to test every spirit, and keep me faithful to the confession that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh. Thank You that the One in me is greater than the one in the world. Let me love my brothers and sisters as You have loved me, so that the invisible God is seen in Your people. Perfect Your love in me, and cast out every fear of punishment, so that I may have confidence on the day of judgement — not because of my goodness, but because of Your love. Let me never claim to love You while I refuse to love the brother or sister I can see. I love You, because You first loved me. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Prodigal Son Comes Home","ref":"Luke 15:11-32","relatedVerses":"v. 9-10, 18-19","summary":"A younger son took his inheritance, wasted it in wild living and ended up feeding pigs. He decided to go home and ask to be a hired servant. “But while he was still far off, his father saw him, and was moved with compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him” (Luke 15:20). Before the son could finish his speech, the father called for the best robe, a ring and a feast. The son came home afraid; the father's love cast out his fear. That is the love of verses 9–10 and 18–19: the Father loved us first, and runs to meet us."},{"title":"The Samaritans Meet the Saviour of the World","ref":"John 4:1-42","relatedVerses":"v. 14","summary":"Jesus asked a Samaritan woman at a well for a drink — a Jew speaking to a Samaritan, a man to a woman with a broken past. He offered her living water and revealed that He was the Messiah. She ran to tell her town, and many came to Him. After He stayed with them two days, they said, “Now we believe, not because of your speaking; for we have heard for ourselves, and know that this is indeed the Christ, the Savior of the world” (John 4:42). John, who was there, writes that the apostles have seen and testify that the Father sent the Son as the Saviour of the world."},{"title":"Elijah and the False Prophets","ref":"1 Kings 18:16-40","relatedVerses":"v. 1-6","summary":"In Ahab's day, Israel was led astray by hundreds of prophets of Baal, popular and royally supported. Elijah stood alone on Mount Carmel and proposed a test: let each side call on its god, and the god who answers by fire is God. The prophets of Baal cried out all day with no answer. Elijah prayed a simple prayer, and fire fell from heaven. The people fell on their faces and said, ‘Yahweh, he is God!’ John's call to test the spirits is the same principle: popularity and passion are not proof. Truth is proved by God's own testimony — and in the New Covenant that testimony centres on Jesus Christ come in the flesh."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 John","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Faith That Overcomes and the Certainty of Eternal Life","themeTitle":"“That You May Know That You Have Eternal Life”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Whoever believes that Jesus is the Christ has been born of God. Whoever loves the Father also loves the child who is born of him.","understanding":"πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ Χριστός, “Whoever believes that Jesus is the Christ” — the test of truth; the man Jesus is the Messiah, the Son of God. ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ γεγέννηται, “has been born of God” — γεννάω, from the birth family, perfect tense: faith is the evidence of a birth that has already happened. καὶ πᾶς ὁ ἀγαπῶν τὸν γεννήσαντα ἀγαπᾷ καὶ τὸν γεγεννημένον ἐξ αὐτοῦ, “Whoever loves the Father also loves the child who is born of him” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; γεννάω three times in one verse. Love the Father, love His family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"}]},{"v":2,"text":"By this we know that we love the children of God, when we love God and keep his commandments.","understanding":"ἐν τούτῳ γινώσκομεν ὅτι ἀγαπῶμεν τὰ τέκνα τοῦ θεοῦ, “By this we know that we love the children of God” — γινώσκω, from the know family; ἀγαπάω, from the love family; τέκνον, from the children family. ὅταν τὸν θεὸν ἀγαπῶμεν καὶ τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ τηρῶμεν, “when we love God and keep his commandments” — τηρέω, from the hold family (many manuscripts read ποιῶμεν, ‘do’). Love for God's children is proved by loving God and obeying Him, not by sentiment alone.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For this is loving God, that we keep his commandments. His commandments are not grievous.","understanding":"αὕτη γάρ ἐστιν ἡ ἀγάπη τοῦ θεοῦ, ἵνα τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ τηρῶμεν, “For this is loving God, that we keep his commandments” — ἀγάπη, from the love family; τηρέω, from the hold family. Love is not only feeling but obedience. καὶ αἱ ἐντολαὶ αὐτοῦ βαρεῖαι οὐκ εἰσίν, “His commandments are not grievous” — βαρύς, heavy, crushing: Jesus said, ‘my yoke is easy, and my burden is light’ (Matthew 11:30). The new nature delights in what it once resented.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"loving"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G926","hebrew":"βαρύς","translit":"barýs","english":"grievous"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For whatever is born of God overcomes the world. This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith.","understanding":"ὅτι πᾶν τὸ γεγεννημένον ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ νικᾷ τὸν κόσμον, “For whatever is born of God overcomes the world” — γεννάω, from the birth family; νικάω, to conquer; κόσμος, from the world family: the whole system that opposes God. καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ νίκη ἡ νικήσασα τὸν κόσμον, ἡ πίστις ἡμῶν, “This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith” — νίκη, victory, the only time this noun appears in the New Testament; πίστις, faith. Faith unites us to Christ, who has overcome the world (John 16:33).","words":[{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3529","hebrew":"νίκη","translit":"níkē","english":"victory"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?","understanding":"τίς ἐστιν ὁ νικῶν τὸν κόσμον, “Who is he who overcomes the world” — νικάω; κόσμος, from the world family. εἰ μὴ ὁ πιστεύων ὅτι Ἰησοῦς ἐστιν ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, “but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?” — not self-discipline, not knowledge, but faith in the person of Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":6,"text":"This is he who came by water and blood, Jesus Christ; not with the water only, but with the water and the blood. It is the Spirit who testifies, because the Spirit is the truth.","understanding":"οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ ἐλθὼν δι᾽ ὕδατος καὶ αἵματος, “This is he who came by water and blood, Jesus Christ” — the strongest reading is that ‘water’ refers to His baptism, where He was declared the Son, and ‘blood’ to His death on the cross. οὐκ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι μόνον, ἀλλ᾽ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι καὶ ἐν τῷ αἵματι, “not with the water only, but with the water and the blood” — against teachers who said the divine Christ came on Jesus at baptism but left before the cross. The Son of God really died. καὶ τὸ πνεῦμά ἐστιν τὸ μαρτυροῦν, ὅτι τὸ πνεῦμά ἐστιν ἡ ἀλήθεια, “It is the Spirit who testifies, because the Spirit is the truth” — μαρτυρέω, to bear witness. μαρτυρέω is from the witness family — to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testifies"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For there are three who testify:","understanding":"ὅτι τρεῖς εἰσιν οἱ μαρτυροῦντες, “For there are three who testify” — μαρτυρέω. The Law required two or three witnesses (Deuteronomy 19:15). Some later Bibles, following a few late Latin and Greek manuscripts, add here ‘in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit; and these three are one’; this addition is not found in any Greek manuscript before the fourteenth century, and the WEB rightly omits it. The Trinity is taught throughout Scripture without it. μαρτυρέω is from the witness family — to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"}]},{"v":8,"text":"the Spirit, the water, and the blood; and the three agree as one.","understanding":"τὸ πνεῦμα καὶ τὸ ὕδωρ καὶ τὸ αἷμα, “the Spirit, the water, and the blood” — three witnesses to Jesus: the Spirit (at His baptism and in believers), the water (His baptism) and the blood (His cross). John also saw blood and water flow from Jesus' side (John 19:34). καὶ οἱ τρεῖς εἰς τὸ ἕν εἰσιν, “and the three agree as one” — they give one united testimony.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":9,"text":"If we receive the witness of men, the witness of God is greater; for this is God’s testimony which he has testified concerning his Son.","understanding":"εἰ τὴν μαρτυρίαν τῶν ἀνθρώπων λαμβάνομεν, “If we receive the witness of men” — μαρτυρία, testimony: we accept human testimony every day in courts and in life. ἡ μαρτυρία τοῦ θεοῦ μείζων ἐστίν, “the witness of God is greater” — μείζων, greater. ὅτι αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ μαρτυρία τοῦ θεοῦ ὅτι μεμαρτύρηκεν περὶ τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ, “for this is God’s testimony which he has testified concerning his Son” — μαρτυρέω, perfect tense: God's witness stands. Two words from the witness family meet here: μαρτυρία, testimony — the evidence or witness that is given; μαρτυρέω, to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"witness"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"}]},{"v":10,"text":"He who believes in the Son of God has the testimony in himself. He who doesn’t believe God has made him a liar, because he has not believed in the testimony that God has given concerning his Son.","understanding":"ὁ πιστεύων εἰς τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ θεοῦ ἔχει τὴν μαρτυρίαν ἐν ἑαυτῷ, “He who believes in the Son of God has the testimony in himself” — the external witness becomes an inner conviction by the Spirit. ὁ μὴ πιστεύων τῷ θεῷ ψεύστην πεποίηκεν αὐτόν, “He who doesn’t believe God has made him a liar” — unbelief is not neutral; it calls God a liar. ὅτι οὐ πεπίστευκεν εἰς τὴν μαρτυρίαν ἣν μεμαρτύρηκεν ὁ θεὸς περὶ τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ, “because he has not believed in the testimony that God has given concerning his Son.” Two words from the witness family meet here: μαρτυρία, testimony — the evidence or witness that is given; μαρτυρέω, to testify, to bear witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G5583","hebrew":"ψεύστης","translit":"pseústēs","english":"liar"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"given"}]},{"v":11,"text":"The testimony is this, that God gave to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son.","understanding":"καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ μαρτυρία, “The testimony is this” — μαρτυρία. ὅτι ζωὴν αἰώνιον ἔδωκεν ἡμῖν ὁ θεός, “that God gave to us eternal life” — ζωή, from the life family: the life of God Himself; αἰώνιος, from the time family; δίδωμι, a gift. καὶ αὕτη ἡ ζωὴ ἐν τῷ υἱῷ αὐτοῦ ἐστιν, “and this life is in his Son” — eternal life is not a thing apart from Christ; it is found only in Him. μαρτυρία is from the witness family — testimony — the evidence or witness that is given.","words":[{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"}]},{"v":12,"text":"He who has the Son has the life. He who doesn’t have God’s Son doesn’t have the life.","understanding":"ὁ ἔχων τὸν υἱὸν ἔχει τὴν ζωήν, “He who has the Son has the life” — ζωή, from the life family. ὁ μὴ ἔχων τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ θεοῦ τὴν ζωὴν οὐκ ἔχει, “He who doesn’t have God’s Son doesn’t have the life.” — the dividing line is not religion, knowledge or morality, but whether you have the Son.","words":[{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"}]},{"v":13,"text":"These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life, and that you may continue to believe in the name of the Son of God.","understanding":"ταῦτα ἔγραψα ὑμῖν, “These things I have written to you” — the purpose of the whole letter. τοῖς πιστεύουσιν εἰς τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ υἱοῦ τοῦ θεοῦ, “who believe in the name of the Son of God” — written to believers. ἵνα εἰδῆτε ὅτι ζωὴν ἔχετε αἰώνιον, “that you may know that you have eternal life” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: settled knowledge, not hopeful guessing; ζωή, from the life family; αἰώνιος, from the time family; present tense: you have it now. His Gospel was written that people might believe (John 20:31); his letter that believers might know.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":14,"text":"This is the boldness which we have toward him, that, if we ask anything according to his will, he listens to us.","understanding":"καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ παρρησία ἣν ἔχομεν πρὸς αὐτόν, “This is the boldness which we have toward him” — παρρησία, the confidence of 3:21, now in prayer. ὅτι ἐάν τι αἰτώμεθα κατὰ τὸ θέλημα αὐτοῦ ἀκούει ἡμῶν, “that, if we ask anything according to his will, he listens to us” — αἰτέω, from the pray family; θέλημα, His will; ἀκούω, to hear with favour. Prayer is not bending God's will to ours, but bringing ours to His (Matthew 26:39).","words":[{"strongs":"G3954","hebrew":"παῤῥησία","translit":"parrhēsía","english":"boldness"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"listens"}]},{"v":15,"text":"And if we know that he listens to us, whatever we ask, we know that we have the petitions which we have asked of him.","understanding":"καὶ ἐὰν οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἀκούει ἡμῶν, “And if we know that he listens to us” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family). ὃ ἐὰν αἰτώμεθα, οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἔχομεν τὰ αἰτήματα ἃ ᾐτήκαμεν ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “whatever we ask, we know that we have the petitions which we have asked of him” — αἰτέω and αἴτημα, both from the pray family: the asking and the thing asked. The answer is as good as received when we ask according to His will.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"listens"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G155","hebrew":"αἴτημα","translit":"aítēma","english":"petitions"}]},{"v":16,"text":"If anyone sees his brother sinning a sin not leading to death, he shall ask, and God will give him life for those who sin not leading to death. There is a sin leading to death. I don’t say that he should make a request concerning this.","understanding":"ἐάν τις ἴδῃ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ ἁμαρτάνοντα ἁμαρτίαν μὴ πρὸς θάνατον, “If anyone sees his brother sinning a sin not leading to death” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἁμαρτάνω and ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. αἰτήσει, “he shall ask” — αἰτέω, from the pray family. καὶ δώσει αὐτῷ ζωήν, “and God will give him life” — ζωή, from the life family. ἔστιν ἁμαρτία πρὸς θάνατον, “There is a sin leading to death.” οὐ περὶ ἐκείνης λέγω ἵνα ἐρωτήσῃ, “I don’t say that he should make a request concerning this” — ἐρωτάω, a second word from the pray family: to make a request, as distinct from αἰτέω. The strongest reading in context is that the ‘sin leading to death’ is the deliberate, persistent rejection of Christ shown by the antichrists who left the church (2:19, 22); others connect it with the sin against the Spirit (Matthew 12:31) or with sins God judges by physical death (1 Corinthians 11:30). John does not forbid praying, but does not command it in that case.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"sees"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinning"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"ask"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2065","hebrew":"ἐρωτάω","translit":"erōtáō","english":"make a request"}]},{"v":17,"text":"All unrighteousness is sin, and there is a sin not leading to death.","understanding":"πᾶσα ἀδικία ἁμαρτία ἐστίν, “All unrighteousness is sin” — two words from the sin family: ἀδικία, wrongdoing, injustice, and ἁμαρτία, missing the mark. Every wrong is serious. καὶ ἔστιν ἁμαρτία οὐ πρὸς θάνατον, “and there is a sin not leading to death” — most sins of believers, confessed and forgiven (1:9).","words":[{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":18,"text":"We know that whoever is born of God doesn’t sin, but he who was born of God keeps himself, and the evil one doesn’t touch him.","understanding":"οἴδαμεν ὅτι πᾶς ὁ γεγεννημένος ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ οὐχ ἁμαρτάνει, “We know that whoever is born of God doesn’t sin” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family); γεννάω, from the birth family; ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family, present tense: does not keep on sinning as a way of life (3:9). ἀλλ᾽ ὁ γεννηθεὶς ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ τηρεῖ ἑαυτόν, “but he who was born of God keeps himself” — τηρέω, from the hold family. The WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘himself’; many early manuscripts read ‘him’, which would make ‘he who was born of God’ Jesus Himself, the eternal Son, who keeps the believer (John 17:12). καὶ ὁ πονηρὸς οὐχ ἅπτεται αὐτοῦ, “and the evil one doesn’t touch him” — πονηρός, from the evil family; ἅπτομαι, to lay hold of, to grab.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil one"},{"strongs":"G680","hebrew":"ἅπτομαι","translit":"háptomai","english":"touch"}]},{"v":19,"text":"We know that we are of God, and the whole world lies in the power of the evil one.","understanding":"οἴδαμεν ὅτι ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ ἐσμεν, “We know that we are of God” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family). καὶ ὁ κόσμος ὅλος ἐν τῷ πονηρῷ κεῖται, “and the whole world lies in the power of the evil one” — κόσμος, from the world family; πονηρός, from the evil family; κεῖμαι, to lie, as a child lies in someone's arms: the world is held in the evil one's grip. There are two families and two realms.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3650","hebrew":"ὅλος","translit":"hólos","english":"whole"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil one"},{"strongs":"G2749","hebrew":"κεῖμαι","translit":"keîmai","english":"lies"}]},{"v":20,"text":"We know that the Son of God has come, and has given us an understanding, that we know him who is true, and we are in him who is true, in his Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God, and eternal life.","understanding":"οἴδαμεν δὲ ὅτι ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ ἥκει, “We know that the Son of God has come” — οἶδα, from the know family (and the see family); ἥκω, to have come and to be present. καὶ δέδωκεν ἡμῖν διάνοιαν ἵνα γινώσκωμεν τὸν ἀληθινόν, “and has given us an understanding, that we know him who is true” — διάνοια, the mind, the capacity to understand; γινώσκω, also from the know family; ἀληθινός, genuine, real, as opposed to false gods. οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ ἀληθινὸς θεὸς καὶ ζωὴ αἰώνιος, “This is the true God, and eternal life” — the strongest reading takes ‘this’ as Jesus Christ, the nearest name: Jesus is the true God and eternal life. ζωή, from the life family; αἰώνιος, from the time family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"has come"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Little children, keep yourselves from idols.","understanding":"τεκνία, φυλάξατε ἑαυτὰ ἀπὸ τῶν εἰδώλων, “Little children, keep yourselves from idols” — τεκνίον, from the children family; φυλάσσω, from the hold family: to guard, as a sentry guards a post, as distinct from τηρέω in verse 18. εἴδωλον, an idol: in Ephesus, the temple of Artemis; in John's letter, also every false idea of God that replaces ‘the true God’ of verse 20. The letter ends with a warning that guards everything it has taught.","words":[{"strongs":"G5040","hebrew":"τεκνίον","translit":"tekníon","english":"Little children"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1497","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","english":"idols"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Victory of Faith","verseRef":"v. 4-5","image":"Picture a besieged city, surrounded by enemy armies. Inside, the people are weak and outnumbered. But the gates open, and their King rides out alone — and the enemy scatters. The people inside did not win by their own strength; they won because they belonged to the King who conquered.","meaning":"“For whatever is born of God overcomes the world. This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith” (v. 4). “Who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?” (v. 5). Faith is not a weapon you swing harder; it is the bond that unites you to the One who has already overcome the world."},{"title":"Water and Blood","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"Picture a river in Judea where a crowd watches a man come up from the water as the heavens open and a voice speaks. Then picture a hill outside Jerusalem three years later, where a soldier drives a spear into the side of the same man, and blood and water flow out.","meaning":"“This is he who came by water and blood, Jesus Christ; not with the water only, but with the water and the blood” (v. 6). His baptism declared Him the Son; His death proved He truly came in the flesh and truly died for sin. The Spirit, the water and the blood agree: this is the Son of God."},{"title":"The Certificate of Life","verseRef":"v. 11-13","image":"Picture someone who has been adopted holding an official certificate with their name, signed by a judge and sealed. They no longer wonder whether they belong. When doubts come, they take out the document and read it again: it is settled.","meaning":"“The testimony is this, that God gave to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son” (v. 11). “He who has the Son has the life” (v. 12). “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (v. 13). God's word is your certificate. If you have the Son, you have the life."},{"title":"Guarding the Gate","verseRef":"v. 18-21","image":"Picture a temple in Ephesus, its huge statue of Artemis surrounded by crowds and incense. Then picture a small house nearby where a handful of believers gather, and an old man says to them, ‘Little children, keep yourselves from idols.’ At the door of the house a watchman stands, keeping guard through the night.","meaning":"“We know that we are of God, and the whole world lies in the power of the evil one” (v. 19). “We know that the Son of God has come, and has given us an understanding, that we know him who is true” (v. 20). “Little children, keep yourselves from idols” (v. 21). Having found the true God, guard your heart against every substitute."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Faith, Love and Obedience Together (v. 1-5)","body":"Everyone who believes that Jesus is the Christ is born of God, and whoever loves the Father loves His children; we know we love God's children when we love God and obey Him, and His commands are not burdensome; everyone born of God overcomes the world through faith that Jesus is the Son of God."},{"label":"The Testimony About the Son (v. 6-12)","body":"Jesus Christ came by water and blood, and the Spirit testifies; the Spirit, the water and the blood agree; God's testimony is greater than human testimony; whoever believes has the testimony within, whoever does not makes God a liar; the testimony is that God has given eternal life in His Son: whoever has the Son has life."},{"label":"Assurance and Prayer (v. 13-17)","body":"John writes so that believers may know they have eternal life; we have confidence that God hears us when we ask according to His will, and we know we have what we ask; pray for a brother committing sin not leading to death, and God will give him life; there is sin leading to death, and all wrongdoing is sin."},{"label":"Three Certainties and a Final Warning (v. 18-21)","body":"We know that the one born of God does not keep on sinning and the evil one cannot touch him; we know we are from God while the whole world lies in the evil one's power; we know the Son of God has come and given us understanding to know the true God, and He is the true God and eternal life; little children, guard yourselves from idols."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Faith That Overcomes","paragraphs":["Chapter 5 ties together the three tests of the letter. Truth: “Whoever believes that Jesus is the Christ has been born of God” (v. 1). Love: “Whoever loves the Father also loves the child who is born of him” (v. 1). Obedience: “For this is loving God, that we keep his commandments. His commandments are not grievous” (v. 3). These are not three separate checklists but three expressions of one new birth. And the commands are not heavy, because a new nature loves what God loves. Jesus said, “For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light” (Matthew 11:30).","The result is victory: “For whatever is born of God overcomes the world. This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith” (v. 4). The ‘world’ here is the whole system of pride, desire and unbelief that John has described (2:15–17). Believers overcome it not by withdrawing from it or by sheer willpower, but by faith that unites them to Christ, who said, “In the world you have oppression; but cheer up! I have overcome the world” (John 16:33).","And that faith has a specific object: “Who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?” (v. 5). Not faith in faith, or in ourselves, or in a vague higher power, but faith in Jesus as the Son of God. The person of Christ is always at the centre of John's letter."]},{"heading":"Water, Blood and the Testimony of God","paragraphs":["Verse 6 is one of the more difficult verses: “This is he who came by water and blood, Jesus Christ; not with the water only, but with the water and the blood.” The strongest reading is that ‘water’ means Jesus' baptism, where the Spirit descended and the Father declared Him the Son (Mark 1:10–11), and ‘blood’ means His death. Some false teachers apparently said that the divine Christ came upon the man Jesus at His baptism but left Him before the cross, so that only a man died. John says no: the Son of God came through both the water and the blood. The One who was baptised is the One who died.","John then speaks of three witnesses: “the Spirit, the water, and the blood; and the three agree as one” (v. 8). Some older English Bibles add words here about ‘the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit’ in heaven. That addition does not appear in any Greek manuscript before the fourteenth century and was not part of what John wrote, so modern translations, including the WEB, rightly leave it out. The doctrine of the Trinity rests securely on the whole of Scripture without it.","Then John argues from the lesser to the greater: “If we receive the witness of men, the witness of God is greater” (v. 9). And God's testimony is simple: “The testimony is this, that God gave to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son” (v. 11). “He who has the Son has the life. He who doesn’t have God’s Son doesn’t have the life” (v. 12). Eternal life is not earned, discovered or achieved; it is a gift found only in Christ. To refuse to believe this is to call God a liar (v. 10)."]},{"heading":"That You May Know","paragraphs":["Now John states the purpose of the whole letter: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (v. 13). The false teachers had shaken the church's confidence. John has given tests — truth, obedience and love — not to make believers anxious but to make them sure. His Gospel was written “that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in his name” (John 20:31); his letter is written so that those who believe may know.","That assurance gives confidence in prayer: “This is the boldness which we have toward him, that, if we ask anything according to his will, he listens to us” (v. 14). Prayer according to God's will is not a weakness of faith but its heart, as Jesus prayed, “nevertheless, not what I desire, but what you desire” (Matthew 26:39).","Verses 16–17 apply this to praying for others. If a believer sees a brother sinning, he should pray, and God will give life. But “There is a sin leading to death” (v. 16). Christians have understood this differently. In the context of 1 John, the strongest reading is that it refers to the deliberate, persistent denial of Christ shown by the antichrists who left the church (2:19, 22) — a rejection of the only source of life. Others link it with the sin against the Holy Spirit (Matthew 12:31) or with sins God judges by physical death (1 Corinthians 11:30). John does not forbid prayer, but he will not command it in that case. What he does command is that believers pray for each other."]},{"heading":"Three Certainties and One Warning","paragraphs":["John closes with three ‘we know’ statements. First: “We know that whoever is born of God doesn’t sin, but he who was born of God keeps himself, and the evil one doesn’t touch him” (v. 18). The believer does not live in sin as a way of life, and the evil one cannot seize him. Many manuscripts read ‘keeps him’, so that ‘he who was born of God’ is Jesus Himself, who keeps His people — as He prayed, “keep them from the evil one” (John 17:15).","Second: “We know that we are of God, and the whole world lies in the power of the evil one” (v. 19). There are two families and two kingdoms; believers belong to God, even while living in a world held in the enemy's grip. Third: “We know that the Son of God has come, and has given us an understanding, that we know him who is true, and we are in him who is true, in his Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God, and eternal life” (v. 20). The most natural reading is that ‘this’ refers to Jesus Christ: He is the true God and eternal life. John 17:3 says, “This is eternal life, that they should know you, the only true God, and him whom you sent, Jesus Christ.”","And then, abruptly, the last line: “Little children, keep yourselves from idols” (v. 21). After revealing the true God, John warns against every substitute — the carved idols of pagan Ephesus, and every false image of God that the false teachers offered. Anything we put in the place of the true God revealed in Jesus Christ is an idol. Paul says, “Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry” (1 Corinthians 10:14). The letter that began with what was seen and touched ends with a guard at the door of the heart."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Witness of the Father, the Victory of the Son and the Keeping of His Own","paragraphs":["The water of verse 6 points to Jesus' baptism: “Immediately coming up from the water, he saw the heavens parting, and the Spirit descending on him like a dove” (Mark 1:10), and “A voice came out of the sky, ‘You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased’” (Mark 1:11). John the Baptist testified, “I have seen the Spirit descending like a dove out of heaven, and it remained on him” (John 1:32). The blood points to the cross, where John watched as “one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out” (John 19:34).","God's testimony to His Son (v. 9) is a theme of John's Gospel: “The Father himself, who sent me, has testified about me” (John 5:37), and “It’s also written in your law that the testimony of two people is valid” (John 8:17). The confidence in prayer of verse 14 rests on Jesus' promise: “Whatever you will ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son” (John 14:13).","The keeping of verse 18 echoes Jesus' prayer: “I pray not that you would take them from the world, but that you would keep them from the evil one” (John 17:15), and His promise: “I give eternal life to them. They will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of my hand” (John 10:28). The true God of verse 20 is the One revealed at the end of the Bible: “behold, a white horse, and he who sat on it is called Faithful and True” (Revelation 19:11)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Victory","hebrew":"νίκη","translit":"níkē","strongs":"G3529 (overcome νικάω, nikáō, G3528)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “conquest (abstractly), i.e. (figuratively) the means of success.” νίκη appears only here in the New Testament; its verb νικάω appears six times in 1 John and seventeen in Revelation. Nike was the Greek goddess of victory. John says the believer's victory is not a goddess, nor human strength, but faith — trust in Jesus as the Son of God. The dictionary's phrase is apt: faith is ‘the means of success’, because it joins us to the One who has already won."},{"english":"Testimony / witness","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","strongs":"G3141 (testify μαρτυρέω, martyréō, G3140)","verses":"v. 9, 10, 11","insight":"Strong's: “evidence given (judicially or genitive case).” μαρτυρία and μαρτυρέω appear ten times in verses 6–11. It is courtroom language: evidence that establishes the truth. The witnesses are the Spirit, the water and the blood — and above all God Himself. Our word ‘martyr’ comes from it: those who bore witness to Christ with their lives. God's testimony is not a feeling but a fact: eternal life is in His Son."},{"english":"Make a request","hebrew":"ἐρωτάω","translit":"erōtáō","strongs":"G2065 (ask αἰτέω, aitéō, G154; petitions αἴτημα, aítēma, G155)","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “to interrogate; by implication, to request.” Verses 14–16 use three words from the pray family. αἰτέω is to ask, as a child asks a father; αἴτημα is the request itself; ἐρωτάω is to ask as one making a request of an equal, or to question. John says believers should αἰτέω for a sinning brother, but does not tell them to ἐρωτάω about the sin leading to death. The variety of words shows that prayer is personal, specific and governed by God's will."},{"english":"True","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","strongs":"G228 (understanding διάνοια, diánoia, G1271)","verses":"v. 20; 2:8","insight":"Strong's: “truthful.” ἀληθινός means genuine, real, true as opposed to fake or counterfeit — not merely ‘truthful’ in speech, but the real thing. John uses it three times in verse 20: we know Him who is true, we are in Him who is true, and Jesus Christ is the true God. In a city full of idols, and a church disturbed by false teachers, John ends by pointing to the only genuine God — and gives believers διάνοια, the understanding to recognise Him."},{"english":"Idols","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","strongs":"G1497 (keep φυλάσσω, phylássō, G5442)","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “an image (i.e. for worship); by implication, a heathen god, or (plural) the worship of such.” εἴδωλον is from εἶδος, form, appearance: an image that looks like a god but is not. Ephesus, where John probably lived, was home to the vast temple of Artemis. But John's last warning reaches beyond statues to every false god — including false versions of Jesus. φυλάσσω, from the hold family, is a guard's word: stand watch over your heart."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this final chapter the Holy Spirit wants to settle your assurance, strengthen your prayers and guard your heart.","items":[{"title":"Do You Know?","body":"John wrote “that you may know that you have eternal life” (v. 13). The Spirit does not want you living in constant uncertainty. If you believe that Jesus is the Son of God, love His people and want to obey Him, then you have the Son — and “He who has the Son has the life” (v. 12). Take God at His word today."},{"title":"Pray for Your Brother","body":"“If anyone sees his brother sinning a sin not leading to death, he shall ask, and God will give him life” (v. 16). The Spirit brings to mind a believer who is struggling, straying or caught in sin. Instead of criticising or gossiping, pray for them — specifically and persistently — and trust God to give life."},{"title":"What Has Taken God's Place?","body":"“Little children, keep yourselves from idols” (v. 21). An idol is anything you trust, fear, love or obey more than the true God. The Spirit asks gently: what has crept into the place that belongs to Him? Money? Approval? A relationship? Comfort? A false picture of God? Name it, and give that place back to Jesus, the true God."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Assurance Check","body":"If someone asked you today, ‘Do you know you have eternal life?’, what would you say? On what would you base your answer (vv. 11–13)?"},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"Do you pray with confidence according to God's will (vv. 14–15)? Are you praying for a brother or sister who is struggling with sin (v. 16)?"},{"title":"The Idol Check","body":"What competes with God for your trust, love and obedience (v. 21)? What false ideas of God or Jesus have you been tempted to accept?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Assurance","body":"Write out verses 11–13 by hand. Underneath, write: ‘I have the Son. Therefore I have the life.’ Then list the evidences John has given in the letter that you see in your own life — faith in Jesus, desire to obey, love for believers, the Spirit's witness. Keep it where you will see it on days of doubt."},{"title":"Smash an Idol","body":"This week, identify one ‘idol’ in your life (v. 21). Take a practical step to dethrone it — fast from it, give it away, confess it to a trusted friend, or replace the time it takes with time in God's word and prayer. Then pray verse 20 aloud: ‘This is the true God, and eternal life.’"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, I believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and I thank You that I have been born of You. Let my love for You show in loving Your children and keeping Your commands, which are not a burden to me. Thank You that the victory that overcomes the world is faith in Your Son. Thank You for the testimony of the Spirit, the water and the blood, and for Your own testimony: that You have given me eternal life, and that this life is in Your Son. I have the Son, so I have the life. Let me know it with settled confidence. Give me boldness in prayer, to ask according to Your will, knowing that You hear me. Help me pray faithfully for my brothers and sisters who stumble. Thank You that the One born of You keeps me, and the evil one cannot touch me. Thank You that the Son of God has come and given me understanding to know You, the true God. Jesus, You are the true God and eternal life. Guard my heart from every idol. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Baptism of Jesus","ref":"Mark 1:9-11; John 1:29-34","relatedVerses":"v. 6-9","summary":"Jesus came from Nazareth to be baptised by John in the Jordan. As He came up out of the water, He saw the heavens torn open and the Spirit descending on Him like a dove, and a voice came from heaven: “You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased” (Mark 1:11). John the Baptist testified, “I have seen the Spirit descending like a dove out of heaven, and it remained on him” (John 1:32). The water of His baptism, the Spirit's descent and the Father's voice all bore witness to who He was — the ‘water’ of 1 John 5:6."},{"title":"Blood and Water at the Cross","ref":"John 19:31-37","relatedVerses":"v. 6-8","summary":"After Jesus died, the soldiers came to break the legs of the crucified men to hasten their deaths. Finding Jesus already dead, they did not break His legs, but “one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out” (John 19:34). John adds that the one who saw it has testified so that you may believe. He was that eyewitness. The real blood of a real death is the ‘blood’ of 1 John 5:6 — proof that the Son of God truly came in the flesh and truly died for sin."},{"title":"Paul and the Silversmiths of Ephesus","ref":"Acts 19:23-41","relatedVerses":"v. 21","summary":"In Ephesus, Paul's preaching turned so many people from idols that the silversmiths who made shrines of Artemis lost business. Their leader, Demetrius, stirred up a riot, and the whole city rushed into the theatre shouting for two hours, ‘Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!’ Many years later John lived in the same city, among the same temples. His last line to the churches of Asia — ‘Little children, keep yourselves from idols’ — was not abstract. The true God had come in Jesus Christ; every other god, however great its temple, was a lie."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"1 John","titleEmojis":"💡❤️","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Walk in the Light, Love One Another, and Know That You Have Life","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 1 John in one overarching thought, it is this: the eternal life that was with the Father has been revealed in Jesus Christ, seen, heard and touched by eyewitnesses, so that we may have fellowship with God, who is light and love; and those who truly have this life show it by believing that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh, by obeying His commands and by loving one another — and so they may know, with settled confidence, that they have eternal life.","paragraphs":["First John is a letter from an old pastor to churches he loves. He calls them ‘little children’ again and again. Some people had left their fellowship, claiming a higher knowledge of God, denying that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh, and saying that sin did not matter. Those who remained were shaken: Were they the ones who had got it wrong? Did they really know God?","John answers by going back to the beginning — to what he had seen with his own eyes. “That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life” (1 John 1:1). The life of God is not a secret idea for the spiritual elite; it came in a real body and was handled by real hands. Fellowship with God comes through Jesus Christ, His Son, and through nothing else.","Then John gives three tests, which he repeats in widening circles through the whole letter. The test of truth: do you believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, come in the flesh? The test of obedience: do you keep His commands and walk as He walked? The test of love: do you love your brothers and sisters, not only in words but in deeds? These are not hoops to jump through but the family likeness of those who have been born of God.","And his purpose is not to make believers anxious but to make them sure: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (1 John 5:13). The letter that begins with what was seen ends with what is known: “We know that the Son of God has come, and has given us an understanding, that we know him who is true” (1 John 5:20)."]},"map":{"intro":"1 John is the twenty-third book of the New Testament, the fourth of the letters written to the whole church. It stands between 2 Peter and the two short letters of John, and it shares the language and themes of John's Gospel — light and darkness, truth and lies, life, love, remaining and knowing.","sections":[{"title":"From Peter's Warning to John's Tests","body":"2 Peter warned that false teachers would arise within the church; in 1 John they have arisen and gone out. Peter called believers to grow in the knowledge of Christ; John shows what true knowledge of God looks like: it believes, it obeys and it loves. Peter looked ahead to the day of the Lord; John calls the present ‘the final hour’ (2:18) and urges believers to remain in Christ so they will not be ashamed at His coming (2:28)."},{"title":"The Gospel and the Letter","body":"John's Gospel was written “that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in his name” (John 20:31). His first letter was written so that those who believe may know that they have that life (5:13). The Gospel tells the story of the Word made flesh; the letter defends that truth against those who denied it and draws out what it means for daily life. The letter names no author, but its language is so close to the Gospel that the church has always read it as the apostle John's."},{"title":"Hard Passages Read Honestly","body":"Several passages need care. ‘If we say that we have no sin’ (1:8) and ‘Whoever is born of God doesn’t commit sin’ (3:9) are reconciled by the Greek present tense: believers still sin and confess it, but do not live in sin as a settled way of life. ‘The atoning sacrifice … for the whole world’ (2:2) is read by some as God's provision offered to all, by others as Christ's sacrifice for people of every nation. The ‘anointing’ (2:20, 27) is the Holy Spirit, not a rejection of teachers. ‘Water and blood’ (5:6) is best read as Christ's baptism and death; the words about three witnesses in heaven found in some older Bibles (5:7) are a late addition not found in any early Greek manuscript. And the ‘sin leading to death’ (5:16) is best read, in context, as the persistent rejection of Christ shown by those who left — though Christians have understood it in other ways too."},{"title":"Word Families in 1 John","body":"1 John's key family is the know family: γινώσκω, knowing by experience and relationship (2:3; 4:8), and οἶδα, settled knowledge of a fact (3:2; 5:13, 18–20). Two new families were added. The darkness family sets σκότος (1:6) beside σκοτία (1:5; 2:8–11). The children family contrasts τεκνίον, ‘little children’ born into the family (2:1), παιδίον, little ones under instruction (2:18), and τέκνον, children of God (3:1–2). Other contrasts include three words for life — ζωή, the life of God (1:2; 5:11–12), ψυχή, the life Christ laid down (3:16), and βίος, the world's goods (3:17); ἁμαρτία and ἀνομία (3:4); and in the pray family αἰτέω, αἴτημα and ἐρωτάω (5:14–16)."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: 1 John ended with the assurance of eternal life and a warning to guard against idols. The next two books are 2 John, a short letter to ‘the chosen lady and her children’ urging them to walk in truth and love and not to welcome those who deny that Jesus came in the flesh; and 3 John, a personal letter to Gaius about welcoming faithful workers for the truth, and about a church leader who refused them.","books":[{"book":"2 John","connection":"2 John is 1 John in miniature, applied to one household church. The same author writes as “The elder, to the chosen lady and her children, whom I love in truth” (2 John 1:1). The same command returns: “Now I beg you, dear lady, not as though I wrote to you a new commandment, but that which we had from the beginning, that we love one another” (2 John 1:5). The same definition of love: “This is love, that we should walk according to his commandments” (2 John 1:6). And the same danger: “For many deceivers have gone out into the world, those who don’t confess that Jesus Christ came in the flesh” (2 John 1:7). Watch how John holds truth and love together, and how he guards the door of the church against false teachers."},{"book":"3 John","connection":"3 John turns from the false teachers who must not be welcomed to the true workers who must be. The elder writes to “Gaius the beloved, whom I love in truth” (3 John 1:1), and his joy echoes the ‘little children’ of 1 John: “I have no greater joy than this: to hear about my children walking in truth” (3 John 1:4). He warns against a leader “who loves to be first among them” (3 John 1:9), and he restates the test of 1 John 3:10 in one line: “He who does good is of God. He who does evil hasn’t seen God” (3 John 1:11). Watch for the practical side of love: hospitality, support for missionaries and humble leadership."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"The Word of Life Revealed","anchorRef":"1 John 1:1-4","body":"The eternal life that was with the Father was revealed in Jesus Christ, and the apostles saw, heard and touched Him. They declare Him so that we may share their fellowship, which is “with the Father, and with his Son, Jesus Christ” (1 John 1:3). Christianity rests on history, not on secret knowledge or private experience."},{"title":"Walking in the Light","anchorRef":"1 John 1:5-2:2","body":"“God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (1 John 1:5). Fellowship with Him means walking in the light — not sinlessness, but honesty. Those who deny their sin deceive themselves; those who confess it find Him faithful: “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness” (1 John 1:9). And when we sin, Jesus Christ the righteous is our Counselor and the atoning sacrifice for our sins."},{"title":"The Tests of True Faith","anchorRef":"1 John 2:3-29; 3:23-24","body":"True knowledge of God shows itself in obedience: “This is how we know that we know him: if we keep his commandments” (1 John 2:3). It refuses to love the world, which is passing away. And it holds to the truth about Jesus: “Who is the liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ?” (1 John 2:22). Belief, obedience and love are not three separate tests but one life, summed up in “that we should believe in the name of his Son, Jesus Christ, and love one another” (1 John 3:23)."},{"title":"Children of God","anchorRef":"1 John 3:1-10","body":"“See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God!” (1 John 3:1). Believers are born of God, and His seed remains in them; they are already His children, and when Christ appears they will be like Him. So they purify themselves, and they do not live in sin, for the Son of God came “that he might destroy the works of the devil” (1 John 3:8)."},{"title":"God Is Love","anchorRef":"1 John 3:11-18; 4:7-21","body":"Love is defined not by our feelings but by the cross: “By this we know love, because he laid down his life for us” (1 John 3:16). “He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love” (1 John 4:8). His love, perfected in us, drives out fear, and it shows itself in loving our brothers and sisters in deed and truth. “We love him, because he first loved us” (1 John 4:19)."},{"title":"Testing the Spirits","anchorRef":"1 John 4:1-6","body":"Not every spiritual claim comes from God: “Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world” (1 John 4:1). The test is the confession that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh. And believers need not fear: “greater is he who is in you than he who is in the world” (1 John 4:4)."},{"title":"Faith, Assurance and Prayer","anchorRef":"1 John 5:1-21","body":"Everyone born of God overcomes the world through faith in Jesus as the Son of God. God has testified that eternal life is in His Son: “He who has the Son has the life” (1 John 5:12). So believers may know that they have eternal life, pray with confidence according to His will, and rest in the One who keeps them — while guarding themselves from every idol."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 1 John practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":"1","title":"Walk in the Light Every Day","lesson":"The Lesson: “But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (1 John 1:7).","practice":"The Practice: End each day this month with an honest review. Ask the Spirit to show you where you walked in darkness — a lie, a grudge, a hidden habit. Confess it specifically to God, using 1:9, and thank Him for His faithful forgiveness. Once a week, share one struggle honestly with a trusted believer, so that your fellowship is in the light."},{"step":"2","title":"Love in Deed and Truth","lesson":"The Lesson: “My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth” (1 John 3:18).","practice":"The Practice: Choose one brother or sister in Christ who has a real need — practical, financial, emotional or spiritual. This week, meet part of that need in a concrete way, quietly. Then name one believer you find hard to love, and pray for them by name every day for a month, asking God to show you one act of love you can do for them."},{"step":"3","title":"Know, and Keep Yourself from Idols","lesson":"The Lesson: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (1 John 5:13); “Little children, keep yourselves from idols” (1 John 5:21).","practice":"The Practice: Write down the three tests of 1 John — belief in Jesus, obedience to His commands, love for His people — and beside each, one evidence of God's work in your life. Thank Him for it. Then name one thing that competes with God for your trust or love, and take a practical step this week to put it back in its place."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Lord Jesus Christ, Word of life, I declare that You were with the Father from the beginning and were revealed in real flesh, seen, heard and touched. My fellowship is with the Father and with You. God is light, and in You is no darkness at all, so I will walk in the light. I will not pretend I have no sin; I will confess my sins, and You are faithful and righteous to forgive me and cleanse me from all unrighteousness. When I sin, You are my Counselor with the Father and the atoning sacrifice for my sins. I will keep Your commands and walk as You walked. I will not love the world or the things in it, for the world is passing away, but whoever does the will of God remains forever. I confess that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, come in the flesh, and I reject every spirit that denies Him. See how great a love the Father has given me, that I should be called a child of God — and so I am. When You appear I will be like You, for I will see You as You are. I will love my brothers and sisters, not in word only but in deed and truth, because You laid down Your life for me. God is love; Your perfect love casts out my fear. I love You because You first loved me. Greater is He who is in me than he who is in the world. I have overcome the world by faith in You. I have the Son, and so I have the life, and I know that I have eternal life. I will ask according to Your will, knowing that You hear me. You keep me, and the evil one cannot touch me. You are the true God and eternal life. I will keep myself from idols. In Your mighty name, Amen!”"}});
